window.QUESTION_BANK = {"adf":{"id":"adf","name":"Azure Data Factory (ADF)","icon":"🏭","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Azure Data Factory and is it an ETL or ELT tool?","type":"concept","a":"Azure Data Factory (ADF) is a cloud-based, serverless data integration/orchestration service used to move and transform data at scale.\n• Supports BOTH ETL and ELT: it can transform before load (Data Flows / Databricks) or load then transform in the target (e.g., Synapse).\n• Key building blocks: Pipelines, Activities, Datasets, Linked Services, Integration Runtimes, Triggers.\n• 90+ built-in connectors (on-prem, cloud, SaaS) via copy activity and mapping data flows.\n• Primarily an orchestrator — heavy transformations are usually pushed to Databricks/Synapse."},{"q":"Explain Linked Services, Datasets, and Pipelines. How do they differ?","type":"concept","a":"• Linked Service = connection info (like a connection string): endpoint + credentials/auth (e.g., Azure SQL, ADLS, Blob). Reusable across datasets.\n• Dataset = a pointer to the data within a linked service (table name, file path, folder). Defines structure + location, not the connection itself.\n• Pipeline = logical container of activities that performs the work (copy, transform, control flow). Triggered by a trigger or on demand.\nAnalogy: Linked Service = connection, Dataset = which table/file, Pipeline = the workflow."},{"q":"What are the types of Integration Runtime (IR) in ADF and when would you use each?","type":"concept","a":"• Azure IR — default, fully managed cloud compute for cloud-to-cloud data movement and Data Flow transformations. Auto-resolves region.\n• Self-hosted IR (SHIR) — installed on an on-prem/VM machine for accessing private/on-prem networks (on-prem SQL, file shares). Establishes outbound secure connection, so no inbound firewall ports needed.\n• Azure-SSIS IR — managed cluster to lift-and-shift/run existing SSIS packages (ETL) in Azure.\nRule: on-prem/private network → SHIR; cloud → Azure IR; SSIS packages → Azure-SSIS IR."},{"q":"How does a Self-Hosted Integration Runtime connect to ADF without opening inbound ports?","type":"concept","a":"• Install the IR software on an on-prem/VM machine.\n• Register it in ADF using the authentication key generated by ADF.\n• The SHIR opens an OUTBOUND HTTPS connection to the ADF service (no inbound ports/firewall changes needed).\n• ADF sends activity requests down that channel; SHIR reads/writes data between on-prem and Azure.\n• Once registered it shows as 'Online' in ADF Monitor."},{"q":"What are the different types of triggers available in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Schedule trigger — wall-clock recurrence (e.g., daily 10 PM), periodic jobs.\n• Tumbling Window trigger — time-sliced windows with state/backfill, supports dependencies between windows; ideal for incremental/scheduled loads with retries.\n• Event trigger — fires when an event occurs (e.g., a blob/file arrives in ADLS / Blob created).\n• Manual (on-demand) — run immediately for testing/ad-hoc.\nChoose: periodic → Schedule/Tumbling; file-landing → Event."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Copy Activity copies data from SQL source to ADLS. A new column was added at the source. How do you handle this?","type":"scenario","a":"• If schema mapping is Auto Mapping, extra columns are ignored/mapped automatically — copy still succeeds.\n• Enable 'Allow schema drift' in the sink/data-flow settings so the new column flows through.\n• In Mapping Data Flows, schema drift lets new/changed columns be handled by name at runtime (use 'Allow schema drift').\n• If using explicit mapping, add the new column to the mapping (or parameterize mapping).\n• Best practice: column-driven, parameterized pipelines + schema drift enabled, and land raw data so downstream (Databricks) handles evolution with mergeSchema."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The Copy Activity keeps failing because of a very large file/data size. How do you fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Increase the DIU (Data Integration Units) / parallelism for Copy to speed up and split the load.\n• For files, use binary copy for a straight file-to-file move (no parsing) — fastest.\n• Increase timeout & retry; enable 'Enable staging' or partitioned copy for large sinks.\n• Split the source read using partition options (physical/virtual partitions, dynamic range) to parallelize.\n• Increase the Self-hosted IR nodes/cores if on-prem, or use Azure IR for cloud.\n• Consider ADF only for movement and push heavy transforms to Databricks; land as Parquet/Delta to reduce size."},{"q":"SCENARIO: How do you design a pipeline that loads many tables with different schemas into ADLS? (metadata-driven ingestion)","type":"scenario","a":"• Store a control/config table (or JSON) listing each source table + its parameters (schema, table, target path, watermark column, load type).\n• Use Lookup/Get Metadata to read the list.\n• Wrap a ForEach over the list; inside, use a parameterized dataset (schema/table/path as parameters) and a Copy Activity.\n• Optionally use a Mapping Data Flow with 'Allow schema drift' to handle differing schemas generically.\n• Write raw to a Bronze landing zone (one folder per table) — no per-table code changes needed for new tables."},{"q":"SCENARIO: How would you copy a list of tables from on-prem SQL Server to ADLS?","type":"scenario","a":"• Create a Self-hosted IR (for on-prem access) and link to the on-prem SQL Server.\n• Use a Lookup/Get Metadata activity to fetch the list of tables (from INFORMATION_SCHEMA or a control table).\n• ForEach over the table list → Copy Activity with a parameterized source dataset (schema+table) and parameterized sink path.\n• Set ForEach concurrency + DIUs for throughput; add retry policy.\n• Write to ADLS Gen2 (Bronze) as Parquet/Delta, optionally partitioned by date/table."},{"q":"What is meant by 'degree of parallelism' and 'batch count' (DIU) in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Degree of parallelism — how many activities run in parallel inside a ForEach (max typically 50) or how Data Flows partition work. Higher = faster but more compute/cost.\n• Data Integration Units (DIU) — unit of compute for the Copy Activity (4–256); more DIUs = faster copy for large data.\n• Batch count — used by Data Flows to control the number of parallel processing batches/partitions. Higher batch count = more parallelism (needs more cores).\nTune both to balance throughput vs cost; over-parallelizing can overwhelm the source or IR."},{"q":"When would you use Mapping Data Flows vs a Databricks notebook for transformation in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Mapping Data Flows — code-free, visual Spark-based transforms (filter/join/aggregate/pivot/surrogate key). Good for moderate complexity, no-code teams, small-medium data.\n• Databricks notebook — full-code PySpark/Scala/SQL for complex logic, ML, large-scale processing, custom libraries. More control + performance tuning.\nRule: simple/medium transforms → Data Flows; complex/large/advanced → Databricks. Many shops use ADF only for orchestration and Databricks for all transformation."},{"q":"How do you implement incremental loading in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Maintain a watermark (last loaded max timestamp/ID) in a control table.\n• Use Lookup to read the last watermark; pass it into the source query as a parameter (WHERE modified_date > watermark).\n• Copy only the delta to the target.\n• After successful load, run a Stored Procedure activity to update the watermark with the new max value.\n• Add retry policy + failure handling so the watermark is only advanced on success (idempotency)."},{"q":"How do you optimize an ADF pipeline?","type":"scenario","a":"• Use incremental loads (watermark/CDC) instead of full loads.\n• Parameterize pipelines/datasets (metadata-driven) to reuse and avoid duplication.\n• Tune degree of parallelism, ForEach concurrency, DIUs, and batch/partition settings.\n• Use the right IR (Azure IR for cloud, SHIR for on-prem) and scale SHIR nodes.\n• Avoid unnecessary copies; use binary copy for raw file moves; use Tumbling Window triggers.\n• Enable staging (Sink staging) and partitioned copy for large loads; choose Parquet/ORC/Delta over CSV.\n• Push transforms to Databricks; monitor with Azure Monitor and set alerts."},{"q":"How do you debug or troubleshoot a failing ADF pipeline?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the Monitor tab → pipeline runs → identify the failed activity and read the error message + activity output (input/output).\n• Check the Linked Service (credentials/firewall) and Dataset (path/schema).\n• Verify parameters/variables and the Integration Runtime status.\n• Use Data Flow Debug mode to step through transforms and inspect data shape.\n• Common causes: auth failure, timeout, file not found, schema mismatch, network/permission issues.\n• Enable Azure Monitor / Log Analytics (KQL) for detailed logs and configure failure alerts; retry the activity."},{"q":"Can a Copy Activity fail on a column mismatch?","type":"concept","a":"Depends:\n• Auto mapping — extra/missing columns are ignored; matching columns are copied, so it usually succeeds.\n• Explicit mapping — if required mapped columns are missing, or the sink datatype is incompatible, it fails.\n• Schema mismatch in a strongly-typed sink (e.g., SQL table) can also fail.\nFix: enable schema drift, add/parameterize mapping, or map to a flexible sink."},{"q":"How is Azure Key Vault integrated with ADF for secrets management?","type":"concept","a":"• Create a Key Vault and store secrets (connection strings, passwords, SPN keys).\n• Create an ADF Managed Identity (or service principal) and grant it 'Get/List' secret permissions on the Key Vault.\n• In Linked Services, choose 'Azure Key Vault' as the secret store and reference secrets dynamically (no hard-coded secrets).\n• In Databricks, mount/read secrets via dbutils.secrets backed by Key Vault secret scope.\nBenefit: secrets never appear in pipeline JSON/notebooks; rotate centrally."},{"q":"How does ADF store pipeline logs and how do you monitor at scale?","type":"concept","a":"• Native monitoring: ADF Monitor tab (runs, activity I/O, durations, errors).\n• Diagnostic settings → send logs/metrics to Azure Monitor / Log Analytics workspace; query with KQL.\n• Create alerts on failed runs / long durations → email, SMS, action groups.\n• ADF stores run history ~45 days; export to storage/Log Analytics for long-term retention.\n• Azure Monitor = metrics + alerting; Log Analytics = queryable log store (KQL)."},{"q":"What is a tumbling window trigger and how does it differ from a schedule trigger?","type":"concept","a":"• Schedule trigger — simple recurrence; no built-in state/history; can miss/fire independently of data windows.\n• Tumbling window trigger — fires for non-overlapping, fixed-size time windows (each with a start/end), maintains state per window, supports retry of just the failed window and backfill of historical windows.\n• Supports dependencies on other tumbling windows and 'delay' to allow late data.\nUse tumbling windows for reliable incremental/scheduled loads with reprocessing needs."},{"q":"How do you pass values between pipelines/activities and parameterize environments?","type":"concept","a":"• Pipeline parameters (runtime inputs) and variables (mutable state).\n• Set Variable / Append Variable activities, and @pipeline().parameters / @variables() expressions.\n• Execute Pipeline activity passes parameters to a child pipeline; child returns via @activity('x').output or a stored proc.\n• Global parameters for cross-pipeline constants (e.g., environment, root paths).\n• Use ARM templates / CI-CD to override parameters per environment (dev/qa/prod)."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move data from an on-prem SQL Server to Azure SQL Database daily. How do you set this up?","type":"scenario","a":"• Install and register a Self-hosted IR on-prem for source access.\n• Create Linked Services: on-prem SQL (SHIR) and Azure SQL (Azure IR).\n• Create datasets for source table and target table; add a watermark column for incremental loads.\n• Build a pipeline: Lookup last watermark → parameterized Copy with WHERE modified > watermark → Stored Procedure to update watermark.\n• Add a Schedule or Tumbling Window trigger for daily runs + retry + alerts."},{"q":"What is schema drift vs schema evolution, and how does ADF handle each?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema drift = the source's columns change at runtime (new/renamed/reordered/removed columns) unexpectedly.\n• Schema evolution = intentionally allowing the target schema to change (add/modify columns) over time.\n• ADF: enable 'Allow schema drift' in Mapping Data Flows to handle incoming columns by name dynamically; enable mergeSchema in COPY/sink for evolution.\n• Downstream Deltabricks: use mergeSchema / autoMerge in Delta for evolution."}]},"architect":{"id":"architect","name":"Databricks Architect — Design & Consulting","icon":"🏛️","roles":["architect"],"questions":[{"q":"How would you design a data platform for 10 data teams that need to share data but have different access requirements?","type":"scenario","a":"This is an ORGANIZATION design problem, not just a tech one. Cover:\n• Catalog structure by domain — a central 'enterprise' catalog for shared/curated (Gold) data, plus team-specific catalogs for team-owned data. Teams produce to their own catalog and consume from enterprise + granted peers.\n• Unity Catalog for account-level governance, lineage, and grants to groups (from the IdP), not individuals.\n• Data ownership model — each team owns its domain data (data-as-a-product); clear producers vs consumers.\n• Access requirements — row filters/column masks for sensitive data; GRANTs per catalog/schema.\n• Governance process — a data council for cross-team standards, naming, contracts, and conflict resolution.\n• Self-service enablement — templates, CI/CD, cluster policies so teams move fast safely.\nInterviewers test whether you design for people and process, not just tables."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You inherit a Databricks platform spending $50k/month. How do you reduce it by 40% without breaking things?","type":"scenario","a":"Business-impact, leadership answer (not just tactics):\n1. Attribute cost first — use system.billing.usage + system tables to break spend by workspace/team/job (chargeback model). You can't optimize what you can't see.\n2. Eliminate waste — idle interactive clusters (auto-terminate), oversized clusters, always-on SQL warehouses (auto-suspend), runaway jobs.\n3. Right-size compute — Job clusters vs All-Purpose, autoscaling, Spot instances for batch, instance pools.\n4. Optimize workloads — Photon, AQE, predictive optimization, liquid clustering, eliminate full reprocessing with incremental loads.\n5. Governance — cluster policies to cap sizes, budgets/alerts, and shift accountability to teams.\n6. Track results and communicate savings; iterate. Emphasize 80/20 (a few workloads drive most cost)."},{"q":"How do you approach a large warehouse-to-lakehouse migration?","type":"scenario","a":"Phased, low-risk migration:\n• Assess — inventory workloads, query patterns, SLAs, data volumes, dependencies; identify quick wins and high-risk areas.\n• Choose pattern — lift-and-shift (fast, minimal refactor) vs refactor/re-architect (better long-term). Often hybrid.\n• Land data — ingest into Bronze; model in Medallion; convert to Delta.\n• Workload-by-workload cutover — migrate the least critical/simplest first; validate outputs vs source (reconciliation).\n• Optimize — liquid clustering/Z-ORDER, partitioning, Photon; rebuild BI on Databricks SQL / Direct Lake.\n• Govern — Unity Catalog, RLS/CLS, lineage.\n• Run parallel until confidence; then decommission. Track business outcomes and communicate with stakeholders."},{"q":"How do you architect a high-concurrency, low-latency data warehouse on Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"Focus on the levers that drive concurrency and latency:\n• Compute — serverless SQL Warehouses with autoscaling + Intelligent Workload Management; separate workloads (BI vs ETL vs ad hoc).\n• Data layout — liquid clustering (over Hive partitioning/Z-ORDER) for data skipping; right-size files via predictive optimization; collect stats.\n• Query engine — Photon (vectorized C++ engine), AQE, caching, range join optimization.\n• Modeling — star schema Gold tables, pre-aggregations/materialized views for dashboards.\n• Concurrency — scale warehouses, connection pooling from BI tools, result caching.\n• Governance/monitoring — query history, system tables, budgets and alerts.\nFrame trade-offs: latency vs concurrency vs cost; iterate with real workloads, don't over-provision up front."},{"q":"Describe the Databricks Well-Architected Framework pillars and how they guide design.","type":"concept","a":"The well-architected lakehouse framework has these pillars:\n• Operational Excellence — automation, CI/CD, observability, incident response.\n• Security, Privacy & Compliance — identity, Unity Catalog, network isolation, encryption, least privilege.\n• Reliability — resilience, fault tolerance, DR, idempotency, retries, checkpointing.\n• Performance Efficiency — right-sizing, Photon/AQE, liquid clustering, serverless, workload design.\n• Cost Optimization — job clusters, Spot, auto-terminate, system table cost attribution.\n• Data & AI Governance — catalogs, lineage, quality, sharing.\nUse them as a checklist in reviews and to balance competing concerns (e.g., performance vs cost)."},{"q":"Centralized data platform vs data mesh — how do you decide?","type":"scenario","a":"• Centralized — one team/platform owns pipelines and data; simpler governance, faster to standardize; can become a bottleneck as domains grow.\n• Data mesh — domain teams own and publish data products with self-serve platform + federated governance; scales org-wide but demands maturity, standards, and a strong platform team.\nDecision factors: org size, number of domains, data literacy, governance maturity, need for speed vs control.\nPractical answer: a hybrid — central platform/standards (Unity Catalog + templates) with domain ownership of data products. Databricks enables this via UC catalogs per domain + Delta Sharing between domains."},{"q":"How do you design for disaster recovery and business continuity on Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"• Data — Delta in ADLS with geo-redundant storage (GRS/RA-GRS); replicate to a secondary region; external tables + UC so metadata follows data.\n• Metastore — account-level Unity Catalog supports failover to a secondary workspace/metastore in another region.\n• Compute — reproducible via Asset Bundles/IaC so you can stand up compute in DR region quickly.\n• RPO/RTO — define targets; Delta time travel + copy for point-in-time recovery; streaming checkpoint restart from replicated storage.\n• Testing — run DR drills; document runbooks; automate failover where possible.\nDiscuss cost trade-off: active-active is expensive; often warm standby or backup/restore meets the requirement."},{"q":"When do you choose serverless compute vs classic clusters?","type":"concept","a":"• Serverless (SQL warehouses, jobs, notebooks) — instant start, no infra management, auto-scaling, ideal for BI, short/elastic workloads, and reducing ops overhead; pay only for use.\n• Classic clusters — needed for custom libraries/init scripts, specific runtime/VM types, GPU/ML, VNet-injected/private networking, or when you want instance pools for steady heavy workloads.\nDecision: default to serverless for simplicity and elasticity; use classic when you need customization, isolation, specialized hardware, or predictable cost at constant high utilization."},{"q":"How do you design the DEV / QA / PROD environment topology?","type":"concept","a":"• Workspaces — separate workspaces per environment (isolation) attached to one Unity Catalog metastore (per region).\n• Data isolation — separate catalogs/schemas per env (dev_catalog, qa_catalog, prod_catalog) with grants; never point dev at prod data.\n• Config — parameterize paths, catalog/schema, cluster sizes per env (DAB targets, ADF global params).\n• Delivery — Git + CI/CD (Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions) deploying bundles/jobs with service principals; promote artifacts, not manual changes.\n• Governance — limit prod permissions; enforce cluster policies; separate service principal per env.\nConsider more granularity (feature branches/preview) only if the team is mature."},{"q":"How do you perform capacity planning and cost attribution at platform scale?","type":"concept","a":"• Cost attribution — system.billing.usage joined with system.billing.list_prices and workspace/job metadata; tag clusters/jobs by team/project.\n• Chargeback/showback — dashboards per team so consumers own their spend.\n• Right-sizing — analyze run history, peak vs average utilization, autoscaling behavior; avoid over-provisioning.\n• Forecast — model growth (data volume, users, jobs) and reserve capacity for spikes.\n• Optimize — predictive optimization, Photon, incremental processing, Spot.\n• Govern — budgets + alerts, cluster policies with max sizes, review regularly with stakeholders."},{"q":"What is the Databricks Photon engine and when does it help?","type":"concept","a":"Photon is Databricks' native, vectorized query engine written in C++ that processes data in columnar batches rather than row-by-row (like the JVM-based engine).\n• Accelerates SQL and DataFrame (Spark) workloads — often 2–8x faster, especially scans, joins, aggregations, and wide transformations.\n• Enabled on compatible runtimes; transparent to your code.\n• Helps most when CPU-bound; less benefit for I/O-bound or tiny workloads. Consider cost vs speed trade-off."},{"q":"What is Predictive Optimization?","type":"concept","a":"Predictive Optimization is a Databricks feature for Unity Catalog managed tables that automatically runs maintenance — OPTIMIZE (compaction), VACUUM, and ANALYZE (statistics) — based on observed usage patterns.\n• Removes the need to manually schedule and tune table maintenance.\n• Reduces storage and improves query performance (data skipping) with fewer small files.\n• Operates on UC managed tables; a key 'cost & performance optimization' answer for architect-level design."},{"q":"What is the range join optimization and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"Range join optimization speeds up joins where the predicate is a point-in-interval or interval-overlap condition (e.g., fact.timestamp BETWEEN dim.start AND dim.end — classic SCD2 lookups).\n• Databricks can bin the interval into a range and use a bucket join, giving order-of-magnitude improvements.\n• Requires manual tuning (range level/bins) and is used for large fact ↔ dimension interval joins.\nArchitect relevance: choosing it for SCD2/validity joins instead of exploding joins or UDFs."},{"q":"How do you design an ML/AI platform on Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"Unified data + ML on the lakehouse:\n• Data — Bronze/Silver/Gold with a Feature Store (feature tables, online/offline parity).\n• Experiments — MLflow tracking for params/metrics/artifacts across teams.\n• Training — GPU clusters; AutoML; distributed training; manage dependencies.\n• Registry — MLflow Model Registry for versioning/staging/production with approvals.\n• Serving — Model Serving (serverless endpoints), batch scoring, or feature lookups; monitor drift.\n• Governance — Unity Catalog for models/functions, lineage, and access.\n• For GenAI — RAG with Vector Search, embeddings, Foundation Model APIs, and governed data.\nEmphasize reuse of governed data and closed-loop monitoring."},{"q":"How do you standardize governance across many teams without blocking them?","type":"concept","a":"Federated governance with guardrails:\n• Central standards, local ownership — Unity Catalog gives account-level policy; teams own their catalogs/schemas.\n• Least privilege via IdP groups, not individuals; grant at the highest sensible level.\n• Policies as code — cluster policies, tagging standards, budgets enforced centrally but consumed self-service.\n• Templates/golden paths — CI/CD bundles, pipeline templates, DQ frameworks teams adopt.\n• Sensitive data — column masks + row filters; classification via Purview.\n• Audit & visibility — audit logs, lineage, cost dashboards; a data council resolves conflicts.\nBalance control with developer velocity."},{"q":"A customer insists on an open-source, self-built Spark platform over Databricks. How do you respond?","type":"scenario","a":"Consultative, no hard sell — compare on total cost of ownership and outcomes:\n• Acknowledge strengths of OSS Spark (no license, flexibility, no lock-in with open formats like Delta/Parquet).\n• Surface hidden costs of self-managed — cluster management, upgrades, security, governance, on-call, GPU/DBA effort; slow time-to-value.\n• Databricks advantages — managed + serverless, Photon performance, Unity Catalog governance, Delta/Lakeflow/MLflow, built-in reliability and observability.\n• De-risk — start with a proof-of-concept on ONE workload, measure performance/TCO, then scale what wins.\n• Keep it objective and tied to the customer's business goals; avoid competitor bashing."},{"q":"How do you design security and network isolation for Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"• Identity — Entra ID SSO; SCIM provisioning; groups for access.\n• Network — VNet injection (customer-managed VNet, subnets, NSGs); Private Link/private endpoints for control plane and storage; no-public-IP workspaces; firewall.\n• Data at rest — SSE (Microsoft-managed or customer-managed keys); Unity Catalog + external locations restrict path access.\n• Secrets — Azure Key Vault via Databricks secret scopes; managed identities/Access Connector; no keys in code.\n• Access control — UC GRANTs, row filters, column masks, least privilege.\n• Monitoring — audit logs to Log Analytics, alerting.\nMap each control to compliance requirements (HIPAA/PCI/etc.)."},{"q":"How do you handle data contracts and data quality at platform scale?","type":"concept","a":"• Data contracts — agreed schema/semantics + SLAs between producer and consumer; enforce via schema validation and versioned interfaces.\n• Quality framework — DLT expectations / declarative expectations, or Great Expectations; classify rules as fail / drop / warn.\n• Quarantine — route bad records to a bad-data store with reasons for triage.\n• Observability — DQ metrics dashboards, alerting on breaches, lineage to trace impact.\n• Governance — data owners per domain; contracts reviewed in the data council.\nPrinciple: shift quality left (validate on ingest) and make failures visible and recoverable."},{"q":"How do you migrate from Hive/legacy tables to Unity Catalog?","type":"scenario","a":"• Inventory — Hive Metastore tables, views, permissions, and dependencies.\n• Upgrade — use the UC upgrade wizard / SYNC command to register Hive tables into UC (managed vs external handling).\n• Access — recreate grants; map legacy ACLs to UC GRANTs; validate per group.\n• Paths — ensure external locations + storage credentials cover the data paths.\n• Validate — compare row counts/checksums; test representative queries; monitor for broken jobs.\n• Cut over gradually — per catalog/domain; keep Hive read-only during transition; then decommission.\nCommunicate, automate, and rehearse rollback."},{"q":"Walk through how you would size and choose clusters for a mix of workloads.","type":"scenario","a":"Match compute to workload:\n• BI/SQL — serverless SQL warehouse with autoscaling and IWM; size by concurrency, not volume.\n• ETL/batch — Job clusters (auto-terminate), memory-optimized VMs for joins/aggregations, autoscaling, Spot for cost; Photon for speed.\n• Streaming — always-on job clusters; size for throughput + state; consider serverless.\n• ML — GPU clusters, single-node for driver-heavy work.\n• Guidance — prefer fewer larger workers over many small (less shuffle overhead); iterate with monitoring; use instance pools for warm start; enforce limits via cluster policies.\nTrade-off: performance vs cost vs concurrency."},{"q":"How do you build observability for pipelines and the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Workload monitoring — Databricks Workflows run history, alerts, repair-and-rerun; query history; Spark UI for bottlenecks.\n• System tables — system.billing.usage (cost), system.access.audit (activity), system.lakeflow.* (jobs), lineage — queryable via SQL for dashboards.\n• Logging — diagnostic logs + cluster logs to ADLS/Log Analytics; KQL queries and alerts.\n• DQ + freshness — expectation metrics, SLA/freshness checks, alerting.\n• Lineage — Unity Catalog column-level lineage to trace downstream impact.\nGoal: detect, diagnose, and alert fast; make cost and reliability visible to owners."},{"q":"What is Delta Sharing and where would an architect use it?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Sharing is an open protocol to share live data (and AI assets) across organizations, clouds, or business units WITHOUT copying it.\n• Provider publishes a share (subset of tables/volumes); recipients read via the share — Databricks-to-Databricks or open recipients (Power BI, pandas).\n• Governance and audit remain with the provider.\nArchitect use cases: B2B data exchange, cross-region/cross-org sharing, marketplace, or when copying data is undesirable/expensive. Complements UC (grants) for internal access."},{"q":"How do you present and defend an architecture decision to stakeholders?","type":"concept","a":"Use an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) mindset:\n• Context — the business problem and constraints (budget, SLA, compliance, scale).\n• Options — 2–3 realistic alternatives with trade-offs.\n• Decision + rationale — what you chose and why, tied to business outcomes.\n• Consequences — cost, performance, risk, and what you'd revisit.\n• Communicate in business terms (cost, time-to-market, risk), not just tech; use diagrams.\n• Be open to challenge; show you weighed alternatives. Interviewers want structured trade-off thinking."},{"q":"Why do you want to be a Databricks/Azure Data Architect, and how do you keep growing?","type":"concept","a":"• Motivation — you enjoy end-to-end problem solving, designing scalable platforms, and multiplying impact across teams (not just writing code).\n• Evidence — examples where you designed, optimized, or governed a platform and delivered measurable outcomes.\n• Growth — certifications (Databricks Data Engineer/Architect, DP-203/DP-700), hands-on labs, keeping up with features (Lakeflow, Declarative Pipelines, Unity Catalog, serverless, Fabric), community, mentoring.\n• Value — you bridge engineering and business, reduce cost/risk, and raise the team's standards.\nKeep it concrete and forward-looking."}]},"architecture":{"id":"architecture","name":"Medallion Architecture & Data Platform Design","icon":"🏗️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold)?","type":"concept","a":"Medallion architecture organizes a lakehouse into progressive quality layers:\n• Bronze (raw) — exact copy of source, minimal transformation, append-only, audit trail, Delta format.\n• Silver (cleansed/conformed) — cleaned, validated, deduplicated, standardized, joined; the 'single source of truth' for analytics/DS.\n• Gold (business) — aggregated, enriched, business-ready (KPIs, star schemas) for BI/reporting.\nTypical flow: ADLS → Databricks → Bronze → Silver → Gold → Power BI."},{"q":"What is stored in each Medallion layer and in what format?","type":"concept","a":"• Bronze: raw as-is + metadata columns (load_date, ingestion_timestamp, source_file). Delta (converted from CSV/JSON/Parquet). No business transforms. Path e.g. /bronze/orders.\n• Silver: validated, deduplicated, type-cast, joined, conformed data. Delta. /silver/orders_clean.\n• Gold: business aggregates, KPIs, star-schema fact/dim tables. Delta (or Parquet for serving). /gold/daily_sales.\nAlways store transformed data as Delta for ACID, time travel, schema evolution, and MERGE."},{"q":"Walk through an end-to-end Medallion implementation.","type":"concept","a":"1. Ingest — ADF / Auto Loader / COPY INTO lands raw files into ADLS Bronze as Delta (add metadata columns).\n2. Transform to Silver — PySpark/DLT: cleanse, dedupe, cast types, standardize, apply DQ checks, join reference data; MERGE incrementally.\n3. Build Gold — aggregate to KPI tables / star schema (facts + dims), apply SCD logic, partition by date.\n4. Serve — Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse; ML from Silver.\n5. Govern — Unity Catalog for access, lineage, RLS/CLS; monitor with alerts + audit.\n6. Orchestrate — Databricks Workflows / ADF with retries, parameterization, dev→qa→prod promotion."},{"q":"What data validations do you perform on the Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema/type validation — columns and types match expectations.\n• Null checks on critical columns.\n• Duplicate checks on business/primary keys.\n• Range/domain validation (e.g., age 0–150, valid enums).\n• Referential integrity (child keys exist in parents via left anti join).\n• Record-count reconciliation vs source/threshold.\n• Business rules (end_date >= start_date).\nImplement as DQ framework / DLT expectations; quarantine or fail on critical violations."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Data moves from Silver to Gold. At what stage do you aggregate and why? How do you apply RLS/CLS there?","type":"scenario","a":"• Aggregate in the GOLD layer — Silver keeps granular, conformed data (reusable, single source of truth); Gold is purpose-built for BI with pre-computed aggregates (faster dashboards, smaller scans). Aggregating too early loses reusability.\n• RLS/CLS: apply in Gold (and where sensitive columns exist) using Unity Catalog row filters and column masks so Power BI/Tableau consumers only see permitted rows/columns. Enforce centrally in UC, not in each report.\n• Also restrict access via GRANTs on Gold tables/views."},{"q":"When would you choose batch vs streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — scheduled, high-throughput, latency-tolerant: end-of-day reports, historical/backfill loads, month-end close, large transformations.\n• Streaming — continuous, low-latency: fraud detection, IoT/telemetry, clickstream, real-time alerting/dashboards, CDC propagation.\nMany platforms use a lambda-style hybrid: streaming for speed, batch for corrections/reprocessing."},{"q":"How do you design a pipeline for incremental data loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Track a watermark (max updated_ts/ID) per table in a control table.\n• Extract only records > watermark (predicate pushdown at source).\n• Apply transforms; MERGE into the target (upsert; handle deletes via CDC if available).\n• Update the watermark only after success.\n• Handle late-arriving data (overlap window / reprocess) and ensure idempotency.\n• Schedule/orchestrate via ADF or Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"What is data quality and what are its dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"Data Quality = how fit data is for its intended use. Dimensions:\n• Completeness (no missing required values)\n• Accuracy (reflects reality)\n• Consistency (same across systems)\n• Timeliness (available when needed)\n• Uniqueness (no duplicates)\n• Validity (conforms to formats/rules).\nEnforced via DLT expectations, Great Expectations, or custom PySpark checks; fail fast or quarantine bad records."},{"q":"How do you handle bad/corrupt records?","type":"concept","a":"Never let bad records corrupt trusted layers. Options:\n• Quarantine — write invalid rows to a separate bad-records table/path for triage.\n• Rescue — Auto Loader's _rescued_data column keeps unmatched/corrupt values.\n• Drop/log — expect_or_drop in DLT (log + drop) or filter with logging.\n• Fail fast — expect_or_fail for critical rules (stop the pipeline).\n• Schema handling — mode PERMISSIVE (keeps corrupt as null), DROPMALFORMED (drop), FAILFAST (error)."},{"q":"What is the 'typical architecture' for an Azure data engineering project?","type":"concept","a":"Sources (SQL Server, APIs, CSV, SAP) → ADF (orchestration/ingestion) → ADLS Gen2 → Databricks (PySpark transformations) with Bronze/Silver/Gold Delta layers → serve to Power BI / Synapse.\nCross-cutting: Unity Catalog governance, Key Vault secrets, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics, CI/CD, and orchestration via ADF/Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"How do you decide between partitioning, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning — low-cardinality columns (date, region, country) where each partition is ≥ ~1 GB; enables partition pruning; avoid high cardinality/over-partitioning.\n• Z-ORDER — high-cardinality filter/join columns (customer_id) on existing (often partitioned) tables; re-cluster on OPTIMIZE.\n• Liquid Clustering — modern default for evolving queries; incremental auto re-clustering, no partition maintenance; can replace partition+Z-ORDER.\nDecide by query filter patterns + table size + maintenance overhead."},{"q":"How do you handle schema drift/evolution across the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Detect: compare source vs expected schema (Get Metadata/Validate) or let reads report new columns.\n• ADF: Allow schema drift in data flows; benign schema changes flow through.\n• Auto Loader: schema inference + evolution (_rescued_data for mismatches).\n• Delta: mergeSchema on write / delta.autoMerge; ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN.\n• Design Bronze to be schema-flexible (raw), enforce structure at Silver/Gold.\n• Add alerts on drift so downstream contracts don't break silently."}]},"behavioral":{"id":"behavioral","name":"Behavioral, Project & HR","icon":"💬","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Tell me about yourself.","type":"concept","a":"Structure (present → past → future, 60–90 seconds):\n• Present — current role, core stack (ADF, Databricks, PySpark, Delta, Azure), and what you build (ETL/ELT pipelines, lakehouse, real-time ingestion).\n• Past — key experiences that built your skills (projects, scale, domains).\n• Future — why this role excites you and how you add value.\nKeep it relevant to data engineering; end with a hook for the interviewer."},{"q":"Walk me through a complex data engineering project you built.","type":"concept","a":"Use STAR: Situation → Task → Action → Result.\n• Situation: business problem + scale (data volume, sources, latency).\n• Task: your responsibility/objective.\n• Action: architecture (ADF → ADLS → Databricks Bronze/Silver/Gold), tools, transformations, governance, CI/CD, challenges you solved.\n• Result: quantify (runtime reduced X%, cost saved, records processed, SLAs met, downtime avoided).\nMention one hard problem and how you solved it to show depth."},{"q":"Describe a challenging problem you faced and how you resolved it.","type":"concept","a":"Pick ONE real technical challenge (e.g., data skew causing a 10h job, small-file explosion, schema drift breaking a pipeline).\n• Context and impact.\n• How you diagnosed it (Spark UI, logs, metrics).\n• The fix (AQE/salting, OPTIMIZE/Auto Optimize, mergeSchema/rescued data, idempotent MERGE).\n• Measurable outcome + what you learned / preventive measure.\nShow systematic troubleshooting, not just luck."},{"q":"How do you handle tight deadlines or changing requirements?","type":"concept","a":"• Clarify and prioritize with stakeholders (MVP first, iterate).\n• Break work into deliverables; communicate risks early.\n• Reuse existing frameworks/patterns (metadata-driven pipelines, templates) to move faster.\n• Automate tests/CI-CD to reduce rework.\n• Document assumptions and keep decisions transparent.\nExample: delivered core pipeline first, added optimizations/DQ in later iterations."},{"q":"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a stakeholder/teammate.","type":"concept","a":"Use a professional, data-driven approach.\n• Explain the situation and the disagreement (e.g., aggressive SLA vs cluster cost).\n• How you listened, gathered evidence (benchmarks/cost analysis), and proposed alternatives.\n• Outcome: found a middle ground (tiered data, incremental processing, right-sized clusters).\n• Emphasize respect, evidence, and shared goals — not 'I was right'."},{"q":"Why are you looking for a change / why this company?","type":"concept","a":"• Change: growth (architect-level scope, modern stack, larger scale), not negativity about the current employer.\n• This company: align their data challenges/tech (Azure, Databricks, lakehouse) with your skills; mention something specific (their domain, scale, modernization).\n• Emphasize what you bring and how the role fits your growth.\nKeep it positive and forward-looking."},{"q":"What are your strengths and weaknesses?","type":"concept","a":"Strengths (2–3, with proof): e.g., end-to-end pipeline design, performance optimization (Spark/Delta), ownership/automation. Back each with an example.\nWeakness (1, genuine + improving): e.g., used to over-engineering → now start simple and iterate; or delegation → actively mentoring/assigning. Show self-awareness and a concrete improvement action.\nAvoid clichés and never use a disguised strength."},{"q":"Where do you see yourself in 5 years?","type":"concept","a":"• Short term: master the current stack, deliver high-impact pipelines, deepen cloud/lakehouse expertise.\n• Long term: grow into a lead/architect role — designing scalable data platforms, mentoring engineers, driving standards.\n• Tie it to continuous learning (certifications, new tech like Fabric/Unity Catalog).\nShow ambition that aligns with the company's path."},{"q":"How do you keep your skills updated / how do you learn?","type":"concept","a":"• Hands-on labs and personal projects (build mini lakehouse, streaming demos).\n• Official docs (Databricks, Microsoft Learn), certifications (DP-203, Databricks Data Engineer/Architect).\n• Community: blogs, meetups, forums, release notes.\n• Apply learning at work — e.g., piloted Auto Loader/DLT, shared findings with the team.\nEmphasize applying, not just reading."},{"q":"How do you estimate effort and prioritize tasks in a data project?","type":"concept","a":"• Break work into tasks; estimate by complexity/uncertainty (similar past work, spikes for unknowns).\n• Prioritize by business value, dependencies, and risk (MoSCoW / value vs effort).\n• Reserve buffer for data issues/integration surprises.\n• Communicate timelines, track progress, and re-plan when blockers arise.\n• Automate/testing reduces hidden effort rework."},{"q":"What do you do when you are blocked on a task?","type":"concept","a":"• Investigate first — logs, docs, minimal reproduction (timeboxed).\n• Try known solutions/search; check if others faced it.\n• Communicate early with the team/lead; don't silently stall.\n• Escalate with context: what you tried, the error, what you need.\n• Document the fix for future reference.\nShows resourcefulness + teamwork."},{"q":"How do you handle production incidents / on-call?","type":"concept","a":"• Triage: assess impact (SLA/customers) and severity.\n• Mitigate first (restart/repair-and-rerun/failover) to restore service, then root-cause.\n• Communicate status to stakeholders regularly.\n• Post-incident: RCA, fix the root cause, add monitoring/alerts and DQ checks to prevent recurrence.\nEmphasize calm, communication, and prevention."},{"q":"Describe a time you automated a manual process.","type":"concept","a":"• Situation: repetitive manual task (e.g., manual file loads, hand-written SQL per table).\n• Action: built metadata-driven ADF pipelines / parameterized Databricks notebooks, scheduled with Workflows, added logging and CI/CD.\n• Result: saved X hours/week, reduced human error, freed the team for higher-value work.\nTie automation to reliability and cost savings."}]},"cicd":{"id":"cicd","name":"CI/CD, Orchestration & Production","icon":"🚀","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"How do you implement CI/CD for Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Pipeline (Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions):\n• Source control — notebooks/code in Git (Azure Repos/GitHub) via Databricks Repos/Git folders.\n• CI — run unit tests (pytest/nutter), lint, build artifacts (wheel/JAR).\n• CD — deploy notebooks/jobs via Databricks CLI/REST API or databricks bundle deploy (Asset Bundles); promote DEV→QA→PROD.\n• Use service principals for deployment auth; store secrets in Key Vault."},{"q":"What are Databricks Asset Bundles (DAB)?","type":"concept","a":"DAB is an infrastructure-as-code way to define Databricks resources (jobs, pipelines, notebooks, clusters, permissions) in a databricks.yml bundle.\n• Supports multiple targets (dev/qa/prod) and parameterization.\n• Deploy with: databricks bundle deploy --target prod; run with databricks bundle run.\n• Enables version-controlled, repeatable deployments across environments."},{"q":"How do you orchestrate/schedule Databricks notebooks?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Workflows (Jobs) — native; multi-task jobs with dependencies, retries, alerting, repair-and-rerun; best for production.\n• ADF — pipeline with Databricks Notebook activities chained on success; best when orchestrating across Azure services (ADB+SQL+Functions).\n• dbutils.notebook.run() — simple chaining from a master notebook (pass args, get exit value).\nRecommendation: use Workflows for production scheduling; ADF for cross-service orchestration."},{"q":"Difference between %run and dbutils.notebook.run()?","type":"concept","a":"• %run — runs another notebook INLINE, sharing scope/variables/functions; no parameters (uses widget defaults); no return value. Use to share utility functions.\n• dbutils.notebook.run() — runs in an ISOLATED context; pass a dict of parameters; returns a string via dbutils.notebook.exit(); has a timeout. Use to orchestrate sequential notebooks and pass/return data."},{"q":"How do you promote code across Dev → QA → Prod in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Separate workspaces (or catalogs/schemas) per environment attached to the same UC metastore.\n• Parameterize storage paths, catalog/schema names, and cluster configs per environment.\n• Use Git + CI/CD (Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions) with service principals to deploy bundles/jobs.\n• Environment-specific config files (databricks.yml targets) or ADF global parameters/ARM params.\n• Run tests in QA before promoting to Prod; keep secrets in Key Vault per environment."},{"q":"For Dev/Test/Prod, should you use one metastore or multiple?","type":"concept","a":"Typical: ONE metastore per region, shared across Dev/Test/Prod workspaces; isolate environments using separate CATALOGS (dev_catalog, test_catalog, prod_catalog), schemas, and permissions.\nUse SEPARATE metastores only for strong isolation: different regions, legal entities, compliance boundaries, or separate organizations."},{"q":"How do you optimize cost in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Job clusters (auto-terminate) for production; avoid idle All-Purpose clusters.\n• Enable autoscaling; set auto-termination aggressively.\n• Use instance pools and Spot instances where appropriate.\n• Serverless/SQL Warehouse with auto-suspend for BI.\n• Enable Photon + AQE + predictive optimization to reduce run time.\n• OPTIMIZE/VACUUM to reduce storage/scan; delete temp tables; right-size clusters.\n• Use cluster policies to cap sizes/costs; monitor DBU usage."},{"q":"What are cluster policies?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster policies are admin-defined rules that constrain what cluster configurations users can create (max/min workers, VM types, allowed options, auto-terminate, tags).\nBenefits: cost control, governance, standardization, and self-service within limits. Enforced via Unity Catalog/workspace admin settings."},{"q":"How do you monitor production pipelines and handle failures?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Workflows — run history, task-level logs, notifications/alerts on success/failure/duration; repair-and-rerun for failed tasks.\n• ADF — Monitor tab, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics + KQL, alerts via action groups.\n• Audit tables — track load history/watermarks for traceability.\n• DQ checks — fail fast on critical violations, alert the team.\n• Foundation: idempotent MERGE + checkpoints so retries/recovery are safe."},{"q":"How do you parameterize pipelines for reuse across environments?","type":"concept","a":"• ADF: pipeline parameters, variables, global parameters, parameterized datasets/linked services, ARM template overrides per environment.\n• Databricks: widgets (dbutils.widgets.get), job/task parameters, DAB targets, and config files (paths, catalog/schema) per env.\n• Keep environment-specific values (paths, secrets, hosts) OUT of code; inject at deploy/runtime."}]},"fabric":{"id":"fabric","name":"Microsoft Fabric","icon":"🧵","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Microsoft Fabric?","type":"concept","a":"Microsoft Fabric is an end-to-end, SaaS-based analytics platform that unifies data engineering, data science, real-time analytics, data warehousing, and BI (Power BI) in one experience.\n• Built on OneLake (single, unified lake) using Delta Parquet.\n• Integrates Power BI, Azure Synapse, and Data Factory.\n• Capacity-based SaaS: no infrastructure to manage."},{"q":"How does Microsoft Fabric differ from Azure Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"• Architecture: Fabric is SaaS (unified, one platform); Synapse is PaaS (manual provisioning of pools/workspaces).\n• Storage: Fabric uses OneLake (unified lakehouse); Synapse uses dedicated SQL pools + data lakes (separate).\n• Compute/pricing: Fabric is capacity-based (F SKUs); Synapse is pay-per-query/provisioned.\n• Integration: Fabric natively integrates Power BI/Azure AI; Synapse needs connectors.\nFabric is the newer consolidated experience."},{"q":"What are the core components/workloads of Fabric?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Factory — data integration/pipelines.\n• Synapse Data Engineering — Spark-based lakehouse, notebooks.\n• Synapse Data Science — ML/AI workloads.\n• Synapse Data Warehousing — managed T-SQL warehouse.\n• Real-Time Analytics — Kusto-based streaming/event processing.\n• Power BI — visualization/reporting.\n• OneLake — unified storage underpinning everything."},{"q":"What is OneLake?","type":"concept","a":"OneLake is the single, unified, logically-centralized storage layer for all Fabric workloads (like OneDrive for data).\n• Stores data in open Delta Parquet format.\n• One copy of data, accessed by all engines (Spark, SQL, KQL, Power BI) — no duplication/silos.\n• Supports shortcuts to external storage (ADLS/S3) without copying."},{"q":"What is Direct Lake mode in Power BI/Fabric?","type":"concept","a":"Direct Lake lets Power BI read Delta tables in OneLake directly without importing/caching data, giving near import-level performance with real-time freshness.\n• No data duplication; no scheduled refresh needed for base data.\n• Compare: Import mode (copies/caches data, fast but stale), DirectQuery (queries source live, slower, no cache).\nDirect Lake blends the speed of Import with the freshness of DirectQuery."},{"q":"How does Fabric handle security and governance?","type":"concept","a":"• Authentication: Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD).\n• Authorization: RBAC at workspace/item level; Row-Level Security (RLS) and Object-Level Security (OLS) in the semantic model/warehouse.\n• Governance: Microsoft Purview for discovery, cataloging, lineage, classification, and policy enforcement across Fabric.\n• Sensitivity labels/endorsements for items."},{"q":"Describe the Fabric capacity/licensing model and SKUs.","type":"concept","a":"Fabric uses capacity-based licensing: you buy Fabric Capacity Units (CUs) as an F-SKU (F2, F4, F8, ... higher = more compute/storage).\n• Capacity provides fixed compute + storage quotas shared across workloads.\n• Workloads consume the capacity; you can pause/resume to save cost.\n• Premium/Embedded P/EM SKUs can also power Fabric features.\nContrast with per-service/per-query pricing."},{"q":"How does Fabric support real-time analytics?","type":"concept","a":"Fabric Real-Time Analytics (based on the Kusto engine) ingests and processes high-throughput streaming data (Eventstreams/Event Hubs/Kafka) and provides low-latency KQL queries and real-time dashboards.\n• Use Eventstream for no-code ingestion/transformation.\n• KQL databases for fast time-series/event queries.\n• Integrates with Power BI for live dashboards."},{"q":"How does Fabric support ETL/ELT?","type":"concept","a":"• Dataflows Gen2 (Power Query) for low-code ELT.\n• Data Factory pipelines (copy activities + orchestration) for ETL/ELT.\n• Spark notebooks (Synapse Data Engineering) for code-based transforms.\n• Lakehouse/warehouse shortcuts and ingestion for ELT into OneLake.\nYou can mix low-code and code."},{"q":"SCENARIO: How would you migrate an existing data warehouse to Fabric?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Export/convert existing data to Delta Lake format.\n2. Load into OneLake (Lakehouse or Warehouse).\n3. Rework queries for the Fabric Warehouse / Direct Lake mode for performance.\n4. Rebuild BI reports in Power BI using Direct Lake.\n5. Apply governance (Purview), RLS/OLS, and workspace RBAC; validate and cut over.\nConsider shortcuts for existing ADLS data to avoid copying."}]},"delta":{"id":"delta","name":"Delta Lake","icon":"🗄️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Delta Lake / a Delta table and what problems does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that sits on top of Parquet in cloud storage and adds: ACID transactions, a transaction log (_delta_log), schema enforcement & evolution, time travel, UPSERT/MERGE, and unified batch + streaming reads/writes.\nIt brings reliability and warehouse-like guarantees to a data lake (the 'lakehouse'), fixing typical data-lake issues: no ACID, no schema control, slow updates, and small-file problems."},{"q":"Explain ACID properties in Delta Lake and the transaction log.","type":"concept","a":"• Atomicity — a commit fully succeeds or fully fails (no partial writes).\n• Consistency — schema enforcement keeps data valid.\n• Isolation — snapshot isolation for concurrent reads/writes.\n• Durability — committed data persists even after failures.\nThe _delta_log is an ordered set of JSON commits recording every change (add/remove files, metadata, protocol). Each read reconstructs the table state from the log; the log is the source of truth and enables time travel and atomic operations."},{"q":"What are the file types inside the _delta_log folder?","type":"concept","a":"• .json — incremental transaction files, one per commit (AddFile, RemoveFile, metadata, protocol). No actual data.\n• .checkpoint.parquet — a snapshot of table state every ~10 commits so readers don't replay all JSON files.\n• .compacted.json — combines multiple small JSON commits to speed up log reads.\n• .crc — checksum/integrity file for checkpoints; on corruption Spark falls back to the previous valid checkpoint.\nTogether they keep metadata reads fast and reliable."},{"q":"What is Delta table metadata log management and how does it become a bottleneck?","type":"concept","a":"Every commit adds a JSON file to _delta_log; over time this grows and slows query planning. Mitigation:\n• Checkpoints (every 10 commits) collapse state so readers load 1 checkpoint + recent JSONs.\n• delta.logRetentionDuration (default 30 days) — old JSON commits are pruned once a checkpoint exists.\n• Compacted JSON files reduce the number of individual commits.\n• OPTIMIZE compacts the underlying Parquet data files (not the log).\nNote: copying the log elsewhere doesn't fix performance — checkpointing + retention does."},{"q":"How does time travel work in Delta Lake?","type":"concept","a":"Delta keeps every version in the log, so you can query historical snapshots:\n• By version: SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 5 (or option('versionAsOf',5)).\n• By timestamp: SELECT * FROM t TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01'.\n• DESCRIBE HISTORY t shows the commit history (operations, versions, timestamps).\nUse cases: audit, reproduce past results, recover from bad writes (RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF n).\nLimitation: time travel is bounded by VACUUM retention — once old files are removed, those versions are gone."},{"q":"Difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema enforcement (validation) — by default Delta rejects writes whose schema doesn't match the table, protecting data integrity.\n• Schema evolution — you can intentionally allow new/changed columns:\n  – df.write.option('mergeSchema','true').mode('append')\n  – SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true\n  – ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ...\n  – COPY INTO ... COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')\n• overwriteSchema replaces the whole schema (use with caution)."},{"q":"How do you implement MERGE (UPSERT) in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"MERGE matches source to target and conditionally updates/inserts/deletes — the standard upsert pattern for incremental/CDC loads.\nExample:\ntarget.alias('t').merge(incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.id = s.id').whenMatchedUpdateAll().whenNotMatchedInsertAll().execute()\nSQL: MERGE INTO t USING s ON t.id=s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\nSupports whenMatchedDelete, conditional updates, and partial column updates. Requires the join condition to be on key columns for efficiency."},{"q":"What does OPTIMIZE do?","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE compacts many small Parquet files into fewer, larger (~1 GB) files to improve read performance and reduce metadata overhead (fixes the small-file problem).\n• OPTIMIZE table; or OPTIMIZE table WHERE date >= '2026-01-01' (target recent partitions).\n• OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY (col) adds clustering for data skipping.\nRun after bulk loads / daily ETL. Target file size is configurable via delta.optimize.maxFileSize. Auto Optimize can do this automatically."},{"q":"What is Z-ORDER and how does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"Z-ORDER co-locates related rows into the same files by reordering data across multiple columns (multi-dimensional clustering).\n• Delta's data-skipping uses file min/max stats to skip files that can't match a filter; good clustering means fewer files to scan.\n• Best on HIGH-cardinality columns used frequently in WHERE/JOIN (customer_id, transaction_id, date).\n• Syntax: OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date).\n• It's expensive — apply to the most queried columns only. Don't combine with auto compaction."},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compacts small files into bigger files; no sorting; always useful for small-file cleanup.\n• Z-ORDER — does the compaction AND sorts/clusters data by column values within files for data skipping; best for high-cardinality filter columns; requires re-running to stay fresh.\n• Liquid Clustering — dynamic, incremental clustering on chosen keys; Databricks re-clusters new data automatically, no partition maintenance, works with evolving workloads; replaces partition+z-order.\nModern guidance: prefer Liquid Clustering; use OPTIMIZE (with ZORDER) on legacy partitioned tables."},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"Liquid Clustering is a Databricks Delta feature that organizes data by clustering keys without traditional Hive partitioning.\n• Define keys once: ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date) (or CLUSTER BY AUTO).\n• Databricks incrementally re-clusters new data; keys can change without rewriting the whole table.\n• Removes partition-column cardinality pitfalls and small-file issues; improves data skipping.\n• No need to run Z-ORDER manually; useful for evolving/changing query patterns."},{"q":"What is VACUUM and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM permanently deletes data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta log and are past the retention period (default 7 days).\n• Reduces storage cost and scan overhead.\n• VACUUM t RETAIN 168 HOURS; add DRY RUN to see what would be deleted.\n• Warning: vacuuming deletes old versions, so you LOSE time travel beyond the retention. Lowering retention below 7 days requires disabling the safety check (risky — can corrupt concurrent readers)."},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed (CDF) and how does it differ from CDC?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF (Delta feature) — records row-level changes (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) in a Delta table so downstream can read changes incrementally.\n  Enable: ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed=true); read with readChangeFeed=true + startingVersion.\n  Extra columns: _change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp.\n• CDC — the broader process of capturing INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from a SOURCE system (via log-based tools like Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, triggers, or timestamp polling).\nIn short: CDC captures changes at the source; CDF propagates Delta table changes downstream. CDF is a Delta-native way to consume changes."},{"q":"What are Deletion Vectors?","type":"concept","a":"Deletion Vectors mark deleted rows in a bitmap rather than immediately rewriting the entire Parquet file.\n• Makes DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE much faster (avoids rewriting whole files).\n• Readers transparently respect the deletion vector; compacted later by OPTIMIZE.\n• Enable via table property delta.enableDeletionVectors=true. Trade-off: slight read overhead until optimized."},{"q":"Difference between DEEP CLONE and SHALLOW CLONE?","type":"concept","a":"• DEEP CLONE — copies the data files AND the metadata/history to a new location; fully independent; supports time travel on the clone; used for migration/backup/experimentation.\n• SHALLOW CLONE — copies only the metadata and references the original data files (no data copy); fast/cheap; if the source files are vacuumed or changed, the clone breaks (depends on source).\nUse deep clone for independent copies; shallow clone for quick test/dev without data duplication.\nSyntax: CREATE TABLE dest DEEP|SHALLOW CLONE source."},{"q":"Difference between managed (internal) and external tables in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — Databricks manages the storage location and lifecycle; DROP TABLE deletes BOTH metadata AND data files. Default format is Delta. Best for lakehouse/DW tables.\n• External table — you specify the storage location (ADLS/S3); DROP TABLE deletes only metadata, data files REMAIN. Shared across tools.\nProduction rule: use external tables (or Unity Catalog managed with careful governance) on ADLS so a drop can't accidentally destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move/copy a Delta table to another location. What options do you have?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read + write new location: spark.read.format('delta').load(src).write.format('delta').save(dest).\n• DEEP CLONE — preserves data + history/metadata to a new path (independent).\n• SHALLOW CLONE — metadata-only, references original files (fast/cheap).\n• Export to Parquet/CSV for external consumption.\nChoose deep clone for a full copy, shallow clone for a quick reference."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A managed table was dropped and, after the retention period, the files were permanently removed. Can you recreate it?","type":"scenario","a":"No — not from Delta alone. Time travel and RESTORE depend on the transaction log AND the underlying data files still existing. Once both are permanently deleted, recovery options are:\n• Restore from backup/DR copy.\n• Reload from the source system and reprocess.\n• Recover via cloud storage versioning/soft-delete if enabled.\nLesson: use external tables (or backups) in production so a DROP doesn't destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The CRC file is corrupted. What happens?","type":"scenario","a":"• The .crc file validates checkpoint integrity. If the CRC check fails, Spark gracefully falls back to the PREVIOUS valid checkpoint and uses the recent JSON commits to rebuild current state.\n• So reads typically still succeed — the corrupted checkpoint is simply skipped.\n• Fix root cause (e.g., partial/interrupted write). Delta is resilient because the log + checkpoints are redundant; you can also force a new checkpoint with OPTIMIZE or Spark actions."},{"q":"What are Delta table optimization techniques?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compact small files.\n• Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering — co-locate related data for data skipping.\n• Partitioning — on low-cardinality columns (date/region) where partitions are ~1 GB+.\n• Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) — automatic file sizing.\n• VACUUM — remove stale files.\n• Deletion Vectors — fast deletes/updates.\n• Predictive Optimization (UC) — automatic maintenance.\n• Right file size (~128 MB–1 GB), avoid over-partitioning, use Photon + AQE, and keep statistics for data skipping."},{"q":"What is the small-file problem and how do you fix it in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"Streaming/micro-batch or many small writes create thousands of tiny files, causing high metadata/listing overhead and slow reads.\nFix:\n• OPTIMIZE to compact into larger files; run on a schedule.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) for automatic compaction.\n• Use trigger(availableNow=True) or larger micro-batches in streaming; consider foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy.\n• Set a target file size and coalesce before writes."},{"q":"How do COPY INTO and Auto Loader differ for ingestion?","type":"concept","a":"• COPY INTO — SQL-based, idempotent batch ingestion of files from cloud storage into a Delta table; tracks already-loaded files; good for scheduled/batch loads.\n• Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — Structured Streaming source that incrementally and continuously discovers new files (file notification or directory listing); scalable, handles schema inference/evolution and rescued data; best for high-volume, near-real-time ingestion.\nUse COPY INTO for simple batch; Auto Loader for continuous/scalable incremental ingestion."}]},"governance":{"id":"governance","name":"Unity Catalog, Governance & Security","icon":"🔐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Unity Catalog and what problem does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance layer for data and AI assets across all workspaces in an account.\n• Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns, rows).\n• 3-level namespace: catalog.schema.table.\n• Built-in data lineage (column-level), audit logs, and Delta Sharing.\n• External locations + storage credentials for governed cloud storage access.\n• Row-level security (row filters) and column masking.\nSolves per-workspace (Hive metastore) silos with account-level governance and fine-grained security."},{"q":"Explain the Unity Catalog object hierarchy.","type":"concept","a":"Metastore (top-level, one per region) → Catalog → Schema (database) → objects (tables, views, volumes, functions, models).\n• Metastore stores metadata + permissions for all workspaces attached to it.\n• Permissions flow downward (grant at a higher level, inherits lower).\n• Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders.\nRelated objects: storage credentials, external locations, shares."},{"q":"Managed vs external tables AND managed vs external volumes in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — UC manages data lifecycle/location; DROP deletes metadata AND data.\n• External table — references a location you own (external location); DROP removes only metadata, files remain.\n• Managed volume — UC-managed storage for non-tabular files (images, PDFs, CSV); deleted with the volume.\n• External volume — references existing cloud storage you manage (via external location); files remain after dropping the volume.\nVolumes are like managed/external tables but for files."},{"q":"What are storage credentials and external locations in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage credential — a UC securable that holds cloud auth (managed identity / service principal / access connector) to access storage; no secrets in code.\n• External location — combines a cloud storage URL (abfss://...) with a storage credential, granting governed access to that path.\n• Access Connector — an Azure resource providing managed-identity access from Databricks to ADLS.\nTogether they let UC govern who can read/write specific storage paths."},{"q":"How do you implement row-level and column-level security in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Column-level: dynamic column masks — a UDF masks sensitive values based on IS_ACCOUNT_GROUP_MEMBER / current_user(): ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_fn.\n• Row-level: row filter functions — ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER filter_fn ON (region); returns only rows the user is allowed to see.\n• Unity Catalog enforces them centrally; also table/column GRANT/REVOKE.\nLegacy: table ACLs / AAD credential passthrough (being replaced by UC)."},{"q":"Difference between Unity Catalog and Hive Metastore?","type":"concept","a":"• Scope: Hive = per-workspace; UC = account-level across all workspaces.\n• Namespace: Hive 2-level (db.table); UC 3-level (catalog.schema.table).\n• Governance: Hive basic ACLs, no column/row security; UC fine-grained (row filters, column masks), lineage, audit logs.\n• Sharing: Hive none; UC Delta Sharing built-in.\n• UC is the current standard; Hive is legacy/being replaced."},{"q":"What is Delta Sharing?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Sharing is an open protocol to securely share data (and AI assets) across organizations/workspaces/business units WITHOUT copying data.\n• A provider shares a subset of tables/volumes; recipients read via the share (Databricks-to-Databricks or open recipients like Power BI/pandas).\n• Governance and audit are maintained; recipients only see what's shared.\nScenario: 3 business units with separate workspaces share data through Delta Sharing instead of duplicating into each workspace."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Three business units have separate workspaces. How do you share data between them?","type":"scenario","a":"Option A — Unity Catalog + Delta Sharing: publish selected tables as a share from the provider workspace; grant recipients access; they read without copying. Centralized governance/audit.\nOption B — one UC metastore per region attached to all workspaces with catalogs per BU and GRANTs (cross-workspace access within the same account).\nChoose Delta Sharing for cross-org/cross-cloud or strict isolation; UC catalogs when all workspaces share an account/metastore."},{"q":"How do you secure access to ADLS from Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Authentication: Service Principal (app registration) + Storage Blob Data Contributor, or Managed Identity / Access Connector (no secrets), or account key (avoid).\n• Secrets: store SPN keys in Azure Key Vault + Databricks secret scope; read via dbutils.secrets.\n• RBAC: Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor/Owner.\n• POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 HNS): file/folder-level rwx with masks + defaults.\n• Unity Catalog: external locations + storage credentials + GRANTs (preferred).\n• Network: private endpoints/service endpoints/firewall.\n• Encryption: SSE at rest (Microsoft-managed or CMK), TLS in transit.\n• Avoid mounting with keys; prefer UC or OAuth."},{"q":"What are service principals, managed identities, and secrets in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Service principal — a non-human identity (app registration) used by pipelines/CI-CD for automated auth.\n• Managed identity — Azure-managed identity so resources authenticate without storing credentials (via Access Connector to ADLS).\n• Secrets — Databricks secret scopes (backed by Azure Key Vault) store passwords/tokens/keys; accessed with dbutils.secrets.get().\nBest practice: least privilege, no secrets in code, rotate regularly."},{"q":"How do you audit who accessed what data in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog audit logs capture user actions (logins, cluster/job runs, permission changes, data access) and column-level lineage.\n• Enable diagnostic logging to a storage account / Log Analytics; query with KQL.\n• Lineage shows how data flows between tables/columns/notebooks/jobs.\n• Combine with Azure Monitor for alerting on suspicious access."},{"q":"What is the difference between RBAC and ACL in the lakehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — permissions granted to roles/principals at the control/service level (e.g., Azure roles on storage, workspace admin roles).\n• ACLs (Access Control Lists) — fine-grained permissions on specific objects (tables, columns, files/folders via POSIX ACLs).\nBoth are used together: RBAC for broad access, UC ACLs/GRANTs for fine-grained data-level control."},{"q":"What is least-privilege design and how do you apply it?","type":"concept","a":"Grant users/apps only the minimum permissions needed.\n• Use groups (not individual users) and grant at the highest sensible level.\n• Use read-only roles where possible; scope service principals to specific storage/workspaces.\n• Apply column masks/row filters for sensitive data.\n• Separate prod/dev credentials; audit and rotate regularly."}]},"modeling":{"id":"modeling","name":"Data Modeling & SCD","icon":"📐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is a data model? Explain star vs snowflake vs galaxy schema.","type":"concept","a":"A data model defines how data is structured, related, and constrained.\n• Star schema — one central FACT table surrounded by denormalized DIMENSION tables. Simple queries, fast aggregations, BI-friendly. Slight redundancy. Most common in data warehouses.\n• Snowflake schema — dimensions normalized into sub-dimensions (less redundancy, more joins, slower).\n• Galaxy / fact constellation — multiple fact tables sharing conformed dimensions.\nExample: Fact_Sales(sale_id,date_key,product_key,customer_key,amount,qty) + Dim_Date, Dim_Product, Dim_Customer."},{"q":"What are the SCD types (0,1,2,3,4,6)?","type":"concept","a":"• Type 0 — never update (static).\n• Type 1 — overwrite the value; no history.\n• Type 2 — add a new row on change; keep full history with start/end dates + current flag (most common in interviews).\n• Type 3 — add a column to store current + previous value only (1 version back).\n• Type 4 — keep current in main table, history in a separate history table.\n• Type 6 — hybrid (1+2+3): full history rows PLUS a current flag and a current-value column."},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 in detail and how you implement it.","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 preserves history by inserting a NEW row whenever a tracked attribute changes; the natural key stays the same, a surrogate key identifies each version.\nColumns: start_date, end_date, is_current (active flag), surrogate key.\nSteps (Delta MERGE):\n1. Expire the current row if tracked columns changed: set end_date = yesterday, is_current = 0.\n2. Insert a new row for the change: start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1.\n3. Insert brand-new keys as-is.\nOnly one active row per business key at any time."},{"q":"CODING: Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark using a hash/flag approach.","type":"coding","a":"Read history + daily data, compute a hash of tracked columns, split into unchanged / updated / new, expire updates, insert new + updates, then union.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, sha2, concat_ws, lit, current_date, when\n\nTRACK = ['name','city']\n\ndef with_hash(df):\n    return df.withColumn('row_hash', sha2(concat_ws('||', *[col(c) for c in TRACK]), 256))\n\nhist = with_hash(history_df)         # has start/end/is_current\nincr = with_hash(daily_df)\n\ncur = hist.filter(col('is_current')==1)\n\n# rows that changed\nupdates = (incr.alias('s').join(cur.alias('t'), 'customer_id')\n           .filter(col('s.row_hash') != col('t.row_hash'))\n           .select('customer_id', *TRACK))\n\n# new keys\nnew = incr.join(cur, 'customer_id', 'left_anti').select('customer_id', *TRACK)\n\n# expire old current rows for changed keys\nexpired = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id').withColumn('end_date', current_date()).withColumn('is_current', lit(0))\nno_change = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\n\n# new versions of changed + brand new\ninserts = (updates.unionByName(new)\n           .withColumn('start_date', current_date())\n           .withColumn('end_date', lit(None).cast('date'))\n           .withColumn('is_current', lit(1)))\n\nfinal = no_change.unionByName(expired).unionByName(inserts)\nfinal.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('dim_customer')"},{"q":"What is incremental loading and what approaches exist?","type":"concept","a":"Incremental loading processes only new/changed data since the last load instead of a full reload.\n• Timestamp/watermark — load where updated_at > last_run_timestamp; track a high-watermark in a control table.\n• CDC — capture inserts/updates/deletes from the source (log-based, e.g., Debezium/Azure SQL CDC).\n• Delta MERGE (UPSERT) — merge changes into the target.\nBenefits: faster, cheaper, less source load. Watch for late-arriving data and reliable watermark updates."},{"q":"Difference between full load and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Full load — reload all data every run; simple; high source impact and long runtime; used for first load/small tables.\n• Incremental — load only new/changed records; needs watermark/CDC; low source impact, fast; for large tables. Risk: must handle late-arriving data and deletes."},{"q":"Difference between CDC and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load — a loading strategy: only bring in new/changed data (often timestamp-based).\n• CDC — a mechanism that captures row-level INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from the source (log-based/trigger-based), so it can also capture deletes and provide before/after images.\nCDC is a superset that feeds richer incremental loads; plain timestamp incremental usually can't detect deletes."},{"q":"Which table loads first, fact or dimension? How do you relate them?","type":"concept","a":"Dimensions load FIRST — facts reference dimension surrogate keys, so the dimensions (and their keys) must exist before the fact rows can resolve those keys.\nRelation: fact tables store foreign keys pointing to dimension surrogate keys; the join is fact.dim_key = dim.surrogate_key.\nOften a 'late-arriving dimension' pattern is used: insert a placeholder (unknown) dimension row if a fact arrives before its dimension."},{"q":"What is the difference between a surrogate key and a natural key?","type":"concept","a":"• Natural (business) key — a meaningful identifier from the source (e.g., customer_id, SSN).\n• Surrogate key — a system-generated integer uniquely identifying each DIMENSION VERSION (used in SCD2 to distinguish versions).\nFacts join to dimensions on surrogate keys; the natural key links versions of the same entity."},{"q":"What are late-arriving dimensions/facts and how do you handle them?","type":"concept","a":"• Late-arriving dimension — a fact arrives referencing a dimension that hasn't loaded yet.\n• Late-arriving fact — a fact arrives after its expected processing window.\nHandling: insert a placeholder/inferred dimension row (unknown key) and update it when the real record arrives; or defer fact loading until the dimension exists; use watermark + reprocessing and idempotent MERGE so late facts are picked up on the next run."},{"q":"What is a slowly changing dimension and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"SCD manages dimension attributes that change slowly over time (e.g., customer address, department). The type chosen controls how (and whether) history is preserved — so reports can reflect either the current value or the value as of a point in time."}]},"pyspark":{"id":"pyspark","name":"PySpark & Coding","icon":"🐍","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the difference between transformations and actions in PySpark.","type":"concept","a":"• Transformations — lazy; build a new DataFrame/plan. Narrow: select, filter, withColumn, union. Wide (shuffle): groupBy, join, distinct, repartition, orderBy.\n• Actions — eager; trigger execution and return results/driver data or write output: count, show, collect, take, write, save, first.\nOnly actions trigger computation; the physical plan is optimized by Catalyst before execution."},{"q":"What is the difference between Python UDFs and Spark built-in functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Built-in functions (pyspark.sql.functions) run natively in the JVM → Catalyst can optimize them, and they work with Tungsten/Photon.\n• Python UDFs run row-by-row, serialize data between JVM and Python → large overhead, can't be optimized by Catalyst, slower.\nPrefer built-ins; when Python logic is unavoidable use pandas UDFs (vectorized, Arrow-based) which are much faster.\nRegister: @udf(StringType()) or F.udf(f, StringType())."},{"q":"Explain window functions in PySpark/SQL with an example.","type":"concept","a":"Window functions compute values across a set of related rows without collapsing them.\n• Ranking: row_number(), rank(), dense_rank().\n• Analytic: lead()/lag() for previous/next rows, running totals with sum() over unbounded window.\nSpec = partitionBy + orderBy (+ rowsBetween).\nUse case: find the latest/max record per group, top-N per category, running totals, month-over-month change.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter('rn = 1').show()"},{"q":"Difference between ROW_NUMBER, RANK, and DENSE_RANK?","type":"concept","a":"• ROW_NUMBER — unique sequential number; ties broken arbitrarily; no gaps.\n• RANK — same rank for ties, then SKIPS numbers (1,2,2,4).\n• DENSE_RANK — same rank for ties, NO gaps (1,2,2,3).\nFor 'top N per group' or finding the Nth salary, DENSE_RANK is usually the correct choice; ROW_NUMBER when you need exactly one row."},{"q":"CODING: Find the highest salary per designation (PySpark).","type":"coding","a":"Use a window ranked by salary descending per designation and filter rank=1. Two common approaches below.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank, max as spark_max\n\n# Method 1: window\nw = Window.partitionBy('designation').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rnk', rank().over(w)).filter(col('rnk')==1).show()\n\n# Method 2: self-join with max\nmx = df.groupBy('designation').agg(spark_max('salary').alias('m'))\ndf.join(mx, (df.designation==mx.designation) & (df.salary==mx.m)).select(df['*']).show()"},{"q":"CODING: Find the second highest salary by department.","type":"coding","a":"dense_rank() = 2 per department. Subquery/self-join alternative also works.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rnk', dense_rank().over(w)).filter(col('rnk')==2).select('dept','name','salary').show()"},{"q":"CODING: Find patients undergoing more than 2 different treatment types.","type":"coding","a":"Group by patient id, count DISTINCT treatments, filter > 2.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import countDistinct, col\nresult = (df.groupBy('pid')\n            .agg(countDistinct('treatment').alias('treatment_count'))\n            .filter(col('treatment_count') > 2)\n            .select('pid'))\nresult.show()"},{"q":"CODING: Get the manager of each employee (self join, handle null manager).","type":"coding","a":"Alias the employee table twice; LEFT join employee to its manager so top-level employees (mgrid null) still appear.","code":"emp = df.alias('emp')\nmgr = df.alias('mgr')\nresult = (emp.join(mgr, col('emp.mgrid')==col('mgr.empid'), 'left')\n            .select(col('emp.empid').alias('employee_id'),\n                    col('emp.empname').alias('employee_name'),\n                    col('emp.mgrid').alias('manager_id'),\n                    col('mgr.empname').alias('manager_name')))\nresult.show()"},{"q":"CODING: Split a comma-separated column into multiple rows.","type":"coding","a":"split() the string into an array, then explode() it into rows.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import split, explode, col\n# Input Col3 = 'G,Y,U' -> rows G, Y, U\nresult = df.withColumn('Col3', explode(split(col('Col3'), ',')))\nresult.show()\n\n# SQL\n# SELECT Col1, Col2, exploded_val AS Col3\n# FROM t LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) tmp AS exploded_val"},{"q":"CODING: Remove duplicate records (keep the latest).","type":"coding","a":"Use ROW_NUMBER over the business key ordered by a recency column (or a dedupe key set) and keep rn=1.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('customer_id','name','email_id','order_date').orderBy(col('load_ts').desc())\ndf2 = df.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter('rn = 1').drop('rn')\ndf2.show()\n\n# Simplest form when rows are identical:\n# df.dropDuplicates(['customer_id','order_date'])"},{"q":"CODING: Calculate the total purchase amount for each customer.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and sum the purchase amount.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import sum as _sum, col\ndf.groupBy('customer_id').agg(_sum('purchase_amount').alias('total_amount')).orderBy(col('total_amount').desc()).show()"},{"q":"CODING: Process a 100M-row CSV efficiently.","type":"coding","a":"Key points: define schema explicitly (avoid inferSchema), use Parquet/Delta, filter early, tune shuffle partitions + AQE, coalesce output.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, sum as _sum\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType\n\nspark = (SparkSession.builder.appName('LargeCSV')\n         .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\n         .config('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions','400').getOrCreate())\n\nschema = StructType([StructField('transaction_id',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('customer_id',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('transaction_date',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('amount',DoubleType(),True),\n                     StructField('category',StringType(),True)])\n\ndf = (spark.read.option('header','true').option('mode','DROPMALFORMED')\n      .schema(schema).csv('/data/large_transactions.csv'))\ndf = df.dropna(subset=['transaction_id','customer_id','amount'])\ndf = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_timestamp('transaction_date','yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss'))\nagg = df.groupBy('category').agg(_sum('amount').alias('total_amount')).orderBy(col('total_amount').desc())\nagg.coalesce(1).write.option('header','true').mode('overwrite').csv('/output/revenue_by_category')"},{"q":"CODING: Convert a Parquet file/folder into a Delta table.","type":"coding","a":"Read Parquet and write as Delta. Use CONVERT TO DELTA (SQL) to convert in place.","code":"# PySpark\ndf = spark.read.format('parquet').load('abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/')\ndf.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_table')\n\n# In-place conversion (SQL)\n# CONVERT TO DELTA parquet.`abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/`;"},{"q":"CODING: Read from Azure SQL via JDBC with an incremental predicate.","type":"coding","a":"Use spark.read.jdbc with a predicates list (pushed down to the source) and write to Delta with MERGE.","code":"jdbc = 'jdbc:sqlserver://srv.database.windows.net:1433;database=db'\nprops = {'user': dbutils.secrets.get('kv','sql-user'),\n         'password': dbutils.secrets.get('kv','sql-pwd'),\n         'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'}\nlast_wm = spark.sql(\"SELECT MAX(last_load_ts) FROM ctrl.watermark WHERE table_name='Customers'\").first()[0]\ndf = spark.read.jdbc(jdbc, 'dbo.Customers',\n        predicates=[f\"ModifiedDate > '{last_wm}'\"], properties=props)\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView('incr')\nspark.sql('''MERGE INTO silver.customers t USING incr s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\n             WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *''')"},{"q":"CODING: Compute a running total (cumulative sum) per group.","type":"coding","a":"Window with partitionBy group and orderBy date, cumulating from unbounded preceding to current row.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import sum as _sum, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('customer_id').orderBy('order_date').rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)\ndf.withColumn('running_total', _sum('amount').over(w)).show()"},{"q":"How do you handle NULLs in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• Drop: df.dropna(); df.dropna(subset=['id','name']); df.dropna(how='all').\n• Fill: df.fillna(0); df.fillna({'salary':0,'dept':'N/A'}); fill with mean/median.\n• Conditional: withColumn('x', when(col('x').isNull(), lit(0)).otherwise(col('x'))).\n• Forward fill: last('col', ignorenulls=True) over a window.\n• Filter: df.filter(col('id').isNotNull()).\n• coalesce(col1, col2) to pick the first non-null."},{"q":"How do you read and flatten nested JSON / structs and arrays?","type":"concept","a":"• Access struct fields: df.select('col.field') or col('col')['field'].\n• Explode arrays: explode(col('arr')) (one row per element) or explode_outer (keeps nulls).\n• Flatten structs: select col('address.*') to promote nested fields.\n• explode map: explode(map_col) → key/value rows.\n• Define schema for nested reads, or let Auto Loader infer; use from_json() for JSON strings."},{"q":"When do you use left anti join and left semi join?","type":"concept","a":"• LEFT ANTI — returns LEFT rows with NO match in the right table (like NOT EXISTS). Use for finding orphans/unmatched records. how='left_anti'.\n• LEFT SEMI — returns LEFT rows that DO have a match in the right (no right columns returned). how='left_semi'. Use as an efficient EXISTS.\nBoth avoid duplicate rows from the right table (unlike a normal join).","code":"unmatched = orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\nexisting  = orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_semi')"},{"q":"How do you pivot and unpivot data in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• Pivot: df.groupBy('dept').pivot('year').sum('salary') — rows → columns.\n• Unpivot/stack: use stack() in SQL or create arrays/maps and explode, or use melt pattern:\n  df.selectExpr(\"id\", \"stack(3,'A',A,'B',B,'C',C) as (key,value)\").\nCommon for turning wide reports into long format for aggregation."},{"q":"What is the difference between union and unionByName?","type":"concept","a":"• union — combines DataFrames by POSITION; schemas must have the same column order/types or you get wrong/NULL results.\n• unionByName — combines by column NAME; align columns correctly regardless of order; use allowMissingColumns=True to fill missing columns with null.\nPrefer unionByName for safety when schemas may differ slightly."},{"q":"How do you create a Spark session and read a DataFrame?","type":"coding","a":"Standard SparkSession pattern; in Databricks 'spark' already exists.","code":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession\nspark = (SparkSession.builder.appName('App')\n         .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true').getOrCreate())\ndf = spark.read.format('parquet').option('header',True).load('/path')\ndf.printSchema(); df.show(5, truncate=False)"}]},"scenario":{"id":"scenario","name":"Scenario-Based & Troubleshooting","icon":"🧩","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"SCENARIO: A pipeline that usually completes in 10 hours is still running into the next day. How do you troubleshoot?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Check the ADF/Databricks Monitor + Spark UI — identify which stage/activity is slow.\n2. Spark UI: look for data skew (one task far longer), large shuffle read/write, spill to disk, uneven partitions.\n3. Compare current vs normal run: input volume growth, small-file explosion, lost broadcast (join strategy change), schema changes.\n4. Check cluster health: resource contention (shared cluster), executor loss/GC, autoscaling limits, pool starvation.\n5. Check upstream source latency/backlog and IR throughput.\nRemedies: AQE/skew handling, salt skewed keys, broadcast small tables, repartition, OPTIMIZE small files, scale cluster, fix bad join, restore filters/column pruning."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A notebook that completes in 15 minutes took 50 minutes today. How do you find the root cause?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the run in Spark UI; find the slow stage and compare task durations → look for skew/spill.\n• Check executor memory/GC and shuffle metrics; look for OOM-ish behavior or spilling.\n• Check cluster: shared usage/contention, autoscaling didn't scale, fewer/larger tasks.\n• Check data: more files/small files, larger volume, changed partitioning, source lag.\n• Check code changes: new join, UDF, missing filter, cartesian, broadcast removed.\n• Fix: repartition/broadcast, enable AQE/Photon, OPTIMIZE, cache reused DataFrames, scale cluster."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Databricks pipeline suddenly fails. What steps do you take to identify and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Open the job run → failed task → read the error/log (driver + executor logs).\n2. Classify: data-related (schema drift, new nulls/bad records), infra (cluster/OOM/network), dependency (upstream table missing), or code (bug).\n3. Reproduce on a small sample; check recent code or source changes (git diff).\n4. Verify upstream data availability, credentials/Key Vault, and table/permission changes.\n5. Apply fix, use repair-and-rerun (only failed tasks) for idempotent pipelines.\n6. Add guardrails: DQ checks, retries, alerts, schema drift handling."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Source data changed structurally (new column added / datatype changed). How do you detect and handle it without breaking pipelines?","type":"scenario","a":"Detect: schema validation step in the pipeline (expected vs actual), or Auto Loader schema evolution / rescued data column.\nHandle:\n• ADF: allow schema drift; parameterize mapping.\n• Ingest raw into Bronze as schema-flexible; add new columns automatically (mergeSchema / autoMerge).\n• For datatype change: cast/map in Silver, keep raw value in Bronze, quarantine incompatible rows.\n• Alert on drift and keep downstream consumers informed; evolve Delta schema explicitly (ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN).\nDesign principle: raw layer tolerant, curated layers strict."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You notice data skew in a large join. How do you confirm and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"Confirm: Spark UI shows a few tasks with much larger shuffle read and longer duration; one executor overloaded.\nFix:\n• Enable AQE skew join (auto-splits skewed partitions).\n• Salt the skewed key (add random suffix), join, then aggregate.\n• Broadcast the smaller table if it fits.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key or by range.\n• Filter reduce before the join; isolate the hot key and process separately."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Delta table has thousands of small files and reads are slow. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Run OPTIMIZE to compact into larger (~1 GB) files; schedule it after loads.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) to prevent small files going forward.\n• Use availableNow trigger / larger micro-batches in streaming or foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Adopt Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy; coalesce before writes.\n• Set a target file size and monitor file counts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Duplicate records are appearing in your Silver table. How do you fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Deduplicate on business key + recency: ROW_NUMBER() over key ordered by load_ts desc, keep rn=1.\n• Use dropDuplicates on the natural key.\n• For incremental loads, MERGE on the key (whenMatchedUpdate) so re-runs don't insert duplicates.\n• Add a DQ check that fails/alerts if duplicates exceed a threshold.\n• Identify the source (idempotency, retries, CDC reprocessing) and fix upstream."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to process millions/billions of records. What's your approach?","type":"scenario","a":"• Store in columnar Delta/Parquet with partitioning/Clustering; avoid CSV/JSON for large scale.\n• Define explicit schemas; filter/aggregate early; select only needed columns.\n• Use broadcast joins for small dims; AQE + skew handling; right-size shuffle partitions.\n• Use Photon; cache reused DataFrames; avoid UDFs.\n• Scale the cluster (workers/VM type) and use autoscaling; use Job clusters.\n• Incremental processing (watermark/MERGE) to avoid full reprocessing."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A memory/OOM error occurs during a large join/aggregation. How do you resolve it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Reduce data early (filter, select needed columns, aggregate before join).\n• Broadcast the small side instead of shuffling the huge side.\n• Increase shuffle partitions to make tasks smaller; use AQE.\n• Fix skew (salting/AQE) so one task doesn't blow up.\n• Use bigger memory VMs / more nodes; avoid collect()/large driver operations.\n• Use persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK) instead of MEMORY_ONLY if needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Compare these two queries and explain when to use each. Q1: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table WHERE salary>5000 GROUP BY emp_id; Q2: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table GROUP BY emp_id HAVING SUM(salary)>5000;","type":"scenario","a":"• Q1 (WHERE) filters individual ROWS before aggregation — only salaries > 5000 are summed per employee.\n• Q2 (HAVING) aggregates ALL rows, then keeps employees whose TOTAL salary > 5000.\nThey give different results. Use WHERE to filter rows cheaply before grouping; use HAVING to filter on aggregate results. You can combine both."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Design a POD-level ADF pipeline that feeds a Databricks job. How do you configure Linked Services, Datasets, and pipeline properties?","type":"scenario","a":"• Linked Services: ADLS (Azure IR), Databricks workspace (with access token from Key Vault), source DB (SHIR if on-prem).\n• Datasets: parameterized source/sink datasets (schema/table/path) so one pipeline handles many tables.\n• Pipeline: Get Metadata/Lookup → ForEach (metadata-driven) → Databricks Notebook activity; pass parameters (run_date, table).\n• Properties: set ForEach degree of parallelism, Copy DIUs, batch count for data flows, retry + timeout, and Tumbling Window trigger.\n• Balance throughput vs cost: use Job clusters, avoid over-parallelizing the source, scale SHIR/DIUs as needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A production job reading secrets from Key Vault starts failing intermittently. How do you diagnose it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Check Key Vault access: is the secret still present/permitted; is the managed identity/SPN expired or the secret rotated?\n• Check the Integration Runtime: is the SHIR online, throttled, or hitting network/timeouts?\n• Check pipeline config: correct secret scope/key, correct vault URI, no hard-coded expiry.\n• Look at error messages (AAD auth vs 403 vs timeout) and correlate with time of day (throttling).\n• Fix: renew/rotate credentials, add retry with backoff, monitor Key Vault diagnostics, add alerts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks cluster fails to start due to resource limitations. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Free up resources: terminate idle clusters and release the quota/cores.\n• Request a quota increase (vCPU/cores) in the subscription.\n• Reduce min workers/VM size, or use instance pools / serverless.\n• Check for subnet/network (no available IPs) or policy restrictions.\n• Retry after cleanup; use cluster policies to prevent oversized requests."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You must do real-time analysis on a streaming dataset in Databricks. How?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read with Spark Structured Streaming from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse JSON (from_json), apply watermark + windowed aggregation, dedupe.\n• Write to a Delta table with checkpointing (exactly-once); use update/append mode.\n• Optionally use DLT streaming tables for managed reliability + expectations.\n• Serve to Power BI/Real-Time Analytics; monitor lag and checkpoint health."},{"q":"What are the common challenges you've faced in pipelines (and fixes)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Data skew → AQE/salting/broadcast.\n• Schema drift → mergeSchema/schema drift/Auto Loader rescued data.\n• Duplicates → dedupe + idempotent MERGE.\n• OOM/memory → filter early, partition, right-size cluster, persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK).\n• Small-file problem → OPTIMIZE / Auto Optimize.\n• Slow large-large joins → broadcast/bucketing.\n• Connection timeouts → retry/backoff, connection pooling, scale IR.\n• Datatype mismatch → explicit schema + validation.\n• Incremental load failures → reliable watermark in a control table.\n• Late-arriving data → overlap window / reprocess."}]},"spark":{"id":"spark","name":"Apache Spark & Databricks Fundamentals","icon":"⚡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the Apache Spark architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Spark uses a master-worker (driver-executor) model:\n• Driver — runs the user code, builds the DAG (logical plan), creates SparkContext, schedules tasks, collects results.\n• Cluster Manager — allocates resources (Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos). In Databricks it's managed.\n• Executors — JVM processes on worker nodes that run tasks and cache partitions in memory/disk.\n• Task — smallest unit of work (one per partition).\n• Stage — set of tasks with no shuffle boundary. Job — triggered by an action.\nFlow: action → Driver → DAG Scheduler splits into stages at shuffles → Task Scheduler sends tasks to executors → results returned."},{"q":"Explain DAG, Jobs, Stages, and Tasks in Spark.","type":"concept","a":"• DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) — the logical execution plan of transformations; built lazily.\n• Job — created when an action is called; a DAG broken at shuffle boundaries.\n• Stage — a set of transformations that can run without a shuffle (narrow pipeline); shuffle starts a new stage.\n• Task — one task per partition per stage; executes on an executor core.\nSo: 1 action → 1+ Jobs → each Job has Stages → each Stage has Tasks (= number of partitions)."},{"q":"What is the difference between narrow and wide transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow — each input partition contributes to exactly one output partition; no shuffle. Examples: map, filter, select, withColumn, union, mapPartitions. Data stays local → fast.\n• Wide — data from many input partitions must be reorganized into output partitions via a shuffle. Examples: groupBy/aggregate, join (non-broadcast), distinct, repartition, orderBy, sort.\nRule: wide transformations cause shuffles → new stages → usually the performance bottleneck."},{"q":"What is lazy evaluation in Spark and why is it useful?","type":"concept","a":"Spark does not execute transformations immediately; it records them and builds a logical plan (DAG). Execution happens only when an action is called.\n• Transformations (lazy): filter, select, join, groupBy, withColumn.\n• Actions (trigger execution): count, collect, show, write, save, take.\nBenefits: the Catalyst optimizer can optimize the whole plan (predicate pushdown, column pruning, join reordering), fewer passes over data, and avoids unnecessary computation."},{"q":"On what basis does Spark create stages?","type":"concept","a":"Stages are split at shuffle boundaries.\n• A stage = a sequence of narrow transformations that can be pipelined together without moving data across the network.\n• A new stage begins whenever a wide transformation (shuffle) is needed — e.g., groupBy, sort, non-broadcast join, repartition.\n• DAG Scheduler creates stages; each stage is submitted as a set of tasks (one per partition) to the Task Scheduler.\nMinimizing shuffles (fewer stages) is a key optimization goal."},{"q":"What is the Catalyst optimizer and how does it optimize queries?","type":"concept","a":"Catalyst is Spark's rule-based query optimizer for SQL/DataFrames. Phases:\n1. Analysis — resolves columns/tables and validates schema.\n2. Logical optimization — rule-based: predicate pushdown, constant folding, column pruning, filter reordering, join reorder.\n3. Physical planning — chooses join strategy (broadcast/sort-merge) and operators based on cost/stats.\n4. Code generation — Whole-Stage CodeGen / Tungsten emits optimized JVM bytecode.\nInspect with df.explain(True) or explain('formatted'). Using built-in functions (not UDFs) lets Catalyst optimize them."},{"q":"What is Tungsten in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"Tungsten is Spark's execution engine focused on CPU/memory efficiency:\n• Off-heap binary memory management (reduces GC overhead).\n• Cache-aware computation and explicit memory layout.\n• Whole-Stage Code Generation — fuses operators into a single optimized function, avoiding virtual function calls.\nResult: much faster execution and lower memory usage than the original Spark engine."},{"q":"What is the difference between RDD, DataFrame, and Dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD — low-level, untyped distributed collection; full control, no schema; no Catalyst optimization; slowest but most flexible.\n• DataFrame — distributed table with named columns + schema; runs through Catalyst + Tungsten; best for structured data (Python/Scala/Java/R).\n• Dataset — typed JVM objects (Scala/Java only); compile-time type safety + Catalyst; Python has no Dataset (uses DataFrame).\nPrefer DataFrames/Datasets over RDDs for performance; use RDDs only for fine-grained control."},{"q":"What are the main Databricks cluster types?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose (interactive) — development, notebooks, ad-hoc; shared; billed while running.\n• Job (automated) — created for a job, terminates on completion; cheapest for production pipelines.\n• High Concurrency — many concurrent users/BI; user isolation; SQL/Python/R (not Scala).\n• Single Node — driver only, no workers; dev/testing/small data.\n• SQL Warehouse — optimized for Databricks SQL/BI (Power BI, Tableau); serverless/pro/classic.\n• Serverless — fully managed compute, no cluster config.\nBest practice: Job clusters for production, All-Purpose for dev, SQL Warehouse for BI."},{"q":"Difference between All-Purpose Cluster and Job Cluster?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose: manually started, stays up (auto-terminate 30–120 min), shared by multiple users/notebooks, warm, higher cost. For interactive development.\n• Job: created automatically from job config, fresh environment each run, isolated, terminated after completion (no idle cost). For scheduled/production jobs.\nUse Job clusters for all production pipelines to control cost and ensure clean, isolated runs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"scenario","a":"• No distributed processing — one machine is both driver and worker.\n• Driver bottleneck — it coordinates AND executes, so it's overloaded.\n• Memory pressure — large datasets/joins can exceed node memory (OOM/spill).\n• CPU saturation — all tasks compete for the same 16 cores; long runtimes.\n• No fault tolerance — if the node fails, the whole workload fails.\nSuitable only for dev/testing/small data — not for production ETL."},{"q":"What is the Databricks Runtime (DBR)?","type":"concept","a":"DBR is Databricks' optimized, curated software stack for clusters. It bundles:\n• Apache Spark (specific version), Delta Lake, and performance optimizations (Photon).\n• Language runtimes: Python, Scala, Java, R, SQL.\n• Optimized libraries and security patches.\nVariants: Standard, ML, GPU, Photon, and LTS (Long-Term Support). Prefer LTS in production for stability/longer support."},{"q":"Difference between repartition and coalesce?","type":"concept","a":"• repartition(n) — full shuffle; can increase OR decrease partitions; even distribution; expensive. Use to fix skew or before heavy joins/aggregations.\n• coalesce(n) — reduces partitions WITHOUT a full shuffle (merges local partitions); cheap; used after filtering to reduce small files. Cannot increase partitions.\n• repartition(n, col) — hash-partition by column to co-locate join keys.\nExample: df.repartition(200, 'customer_id'); df.coalesce(4)."},{"q":"What is the default partition size and default number of shuffle partitions?","type":"concept","a":"• Target partition/file size in Spark/Delta — ~128 MB (block/file size).\n• Default shuffle partitions — spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200.\n• Default parallelism — equals the number of executor cores.\nTune shuffle partitions to data size (too few → huge tasks/OOM; too many → tiny tasks/overhead). AQE can coalesce partitions automatically at runtime."},{"q":"Difference between cache() and persist()?","type":"concept","a":"• cache() — stores the DataFrame/RDD using MEMORY_AND_DISK storage level; lazy (materializes on first action).\n• persist(level) — same but you choose the storage level: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK_SER, etc.\nBoth are lazy transformations; trigger with an action (count/write). Use when a DataFrame is reused 2+ times (iterative ML, repeated joins).\nAlways unpersist() when done to free memory."},{"q":"What is a broadcast join and when is it used?","type":"concept","a":"A broadcast join replicates a SMALL table to every executor so the large table doesn't need to be shuffled.\n• Use when one side is small (< ~10 MB default autoBroadcastJoinThreshold, up to ~200 MB feasible).\n• Eliminates the shuffle of the big table → big performance gain and also mitigates skew.\nPySpark: large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id'); SQL: /*+ BROADCAST(small) */.\nTypical for joining a fact with a small lookup/dimension table."},{"q":"What is data skew and how do you detect and fix it?","type":"concept","a":"Skew = uneven data distribution across partitions so a few tasks process far more data (stragglers).\nDetect: Spark UI shows one/few tasks much longer, uneven shuffle read sizes, one executor overloaded while others idle.\nFix:\n• Salting — add a random prefix/suffix to the skewed key to spread it.\n• Enable AQE skew join (spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true) — splits skewed partitions automatically.\n• Broadcast join for the small side.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key; filter early.\n• Isolate and process the hot key separately.","code":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\nspark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true')\n# Salting\ndf = df.withColumn('salt', (F.rand()*10).cast('int'))\ndf = df.withColumn('salted_key', F.concat(col('key'), F.lit('_'), col('salt')))"},{"q":"What is bucketing and when would you use it?","type":"concept","a":"Bucketing pre-shuffles data into a fixed number of buckets by hash of a column, persisted with the table (e.g., bucketBy(50,'customer_id').saveAsTable(...)).\n• Benefit: repeated joins/aggregations on the bucketed key avoid shuffles (sort-merge join becomes shuffle-free).\n• Differs from partitioning: partitioning creates directories (low cardinality); bucketing creates fixed files (higher cardinality, even distribution).\nUse for large, frequently joined tables on the same key. In Databricks, Liquid Clustering is now often preferred over manual bucketing/partitioning."},{"q":"What is a shuffle and how do you reduce it?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffle = redistribution of data across executors (network + disk I/O) for wide transformations like join, groupBy, distinct, sort.\nReduce it by: broadcast joins for small tables, filtering/aggregating before joins, bucketing on join keys, using map-side partial aggregation (reduceByKey over groupByKey), avoiding unnecessary repartition/distinct, and enabling AQE.\nWatch shuffle read/write metrics in the Spark UI — it's usually the top cost."},{"q":"What is Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) and its benefits?","type":"concept","a":"AQE re-optimizes the physical plan at runtime using actual statistics gathered after each stage. Benefits:\n• Dynamically coalesces shuffle partitions (reduces tiny tasks).\n• Switches join strategies (e.g., sort-merge → broadcast) based on actual sizes.\n• Detects and splits skewed partitions (skew join handling).\nEnable: spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true (plus skewJoin + coalescePartitions). Default on in Spark 3.x/Databricks runtime."},{"q":"What is Photon?","type":"concept","a":"Photon is Databricks' vectorized C++ query engine. It replaces the JVM execution for many SQL/DataFrame operators with native, columnar, vectorized execution.\n• Delivers ~3–10x speedups for SQL/scan/aggregation/join-heavy workloads.\n• Drop-in: enable in cluster config; no code changes.\n• Best for SQL and Delta workloads; some UDF-heavy or non-supported ops fall back to the JVM."},{"q":"How do you use the Spark UI to troubleshoot performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Jobs/Stages tabs — find slow stages; check task durations and counts.\n• Tasks — spot skew (one task far longer), spill to disk, retries.\n• SQL/DataFrame tab — inspect the physical plan, exchanges (shuffles), and per-operator metrics.\n• Storage tab — what's cached and its size.\n• Executors tab — memory/GC, shuffle read/write, disk spill.\nUse these to decide: broadcast, repartition, AQE, more partitions, or bigger cluster."},{"q":"How do you decide the size of a Databricks cluster?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster sizing depends on data volume, number of files, transformation complexity, SLA, and concurrency — it can't be predicted, so iterate:\n1. Start with a medium cluster (2–8 nodes) matching the workload class (F/DSv2 for ETL, DSv2 for ML, L for analytics).\n2. Run end-to-end on representative data; measure CPU, memory, I/O, shuffle.\n3. Remove bottlenecks: CPU-bound → add nodes/cores; network-bound → fewer bigger SSD machines; disk/spill-bound → more memory.\n4. Repeat 3–4 times on increasing data sizes; prefer fewer big instances over many small (1 executor/machine) for batch ETL, enable autoscaling.\nFirst guess: scale linearly from a small test (e.g., 5 nodes for 100 TB ≈ 50 nodes for 1 PB), then refine."},{"q":"What are the key cluster configuration options in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster mode: Single Node / Standard / High Concurrency (or Serverless).\n• Runtime version (prefer LTS, e.g., 15.4 LTS).\n• Driver + worker VM types (workers can be sized independently).\n• Worker count with autoscaling (min/max) or fixed.\n• Photon on/off; instance pools for fast start.\n• Auto-termination (idle timeout).\n• Access mode / Unity Catalog mode (Single user, Shared, No isolation).\n• Cluster policies, tags, init scripts, and Spark configs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks job that normally finishes quickly is now taking far longer. How do you investigate?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the job run → Spark UI; find the slow stage and inspect task times.\n• Check for data skew (one task much longer), shuffle size, and spill-to-disk in the Tasks/Executors tabs.\n• Check input data volume/growth and partition count; look for small-file explosion.\n• Check cluster health — resource contention (shared cluster), GC, executor loss, autoscaling lag.\n• Compare the current physical plan vs a known-good run; check join strategy changes (broadcast vs sort-merge).\n• Look for recently changed code (new joins/UDFs), source schema changes, or upstream backlog.\n• Remedy: tune partitions, enable AQE/Photon, broadcast small tables, salt skewed keys, scale cluster, OPTIMIZE tables."},{"q":"What is the difference between Spark SQL, DataFrame API, and RDD API?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD API — lowest level, no schema, no Catalyst; maximum control but least optimization.\n• DataFrame API — structured, schema-aware, optimized by Catalyst/Tungsten; expressive transforms.\n• Spark SQL — declarative SQL strings over the same engine (also Catalyst-optimized); easy for analysts and interop.\nAll compile to the same optimized plan; use DataFrames/SQL for day-to-day, RDDs only for niche low-level needs."},{"q":"Why is PySpark used and how does it interface with the JVM?","type":"concept","a":"PySpark is Spark's Python API. Spark is written in Scala and runs on the JVM; PySpark uses Py4J to let Python code call JVM objects.\n• Benefits: Python ecosystem (pandas, ML libs), ease of use, distributed processing across a cluster.\n• Note: Python UDFs incur serialization overhead (row-by-row), so prefer Spark built-in functions / pandas UDFs (vectorized) for performance.\n• Needs Java installed alongside Python and Spark for local development."}]},"sql":{"id":"sql","name":"SQL & Query Optimization","icon":"🗃️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows BEFORE grouping/aggregation; cannot use aggregate functions.\n• HAVING filters groups AFTER GROUP BY; can use aggregates.\n• A query can have both: WHERE to reduce rows, then GROUP BY, then HAVING to filter groups.\nExample: SELECT emp, SUM(sal) FROM t WHERE dept='IT' GROUP BY emp HAVING SUM(sal) > 5000."},{"q":"Explain INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, CROSS, LEFT SEMI, and LEFT ANTI joins.","type":"concept","a":"• INNER — only matching rows from both tables.\n• LEFT — all left rows + matches; unmatched right = NULL.\n• RIGHT — all right rows + matches; unmatched left = NULL.\n• FULL OUTER — all rows from both; NULLs where no match.\n• CROSS — cartesian product (every combination).\n• LEFT SEMI — left rows with a match in right (no right columns).\n• LEFT ANTI — left rows with NO match in right.\nLEFT JOIN A,B = RIGHT JOIN B,A."},{"q":"CODING: Total revenue per month.","type":"coding","a":"Group by the formatted month and sum amounts.","code":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM') AS year_month,\n       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM')\nORDER BY year_month;"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in both January AND February 2025.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and count distinct months; keep those with 2. INTERSECT is an alternative.","code":"SELECT customer_id\nFROM orders\nWHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28'\n  AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1,2)\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2;\n\n-- Alternative\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01'\nINTERSECT\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in 3 consecutive months.","type":"coding","a":"Use LAG to look back two months and compare the dates.","code":"WITH m AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) AS mth\n  FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)\n),\nl AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, mth,\n         LAG(mth,1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev1,\n         LAG(mth,2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev2\n  FROM m\n)\nSELECT DISTINCT customer_id\nFROM l\nWHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2,1) = prev1 AND ADD_MONTHS(prev1,1) = mth;"},{"q":"CODING: Total order amount per customer (join + aggregation).","type":"coding","a":"Inner join customers to orders and sum.","code":"SELECT c.CustomerID, c.Name, SUM(o.Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Customer c\nINNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID\nGROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.Name;"},{"q":"CODING: Delete duplicate records keeping one row.","type":"coding","a":"Assign row numbers per duplicate key and delete rn>1 (or rebuild the table).","code":"WITH cte AS (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer\n)\nDELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n\n-- Delta-friendly rebuild\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n     PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer) WHERE rn = 1;"},{"q":"How would you optimize a slow SQL query?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read the execution plan (EXPLAIN) to find the bottleneck (scans, shuffles, spills).\n• Filter as early as possible; avoid SELECT * (select needed columns only).\n• Ensure joins/filters use indexed or partition columns.\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE (e.g., YEAR(date)=2025) — use range predicates for partition pruning.\n• Use proper join types and broadcast hints for small tables.\n• Replace correlated subqueries with joins; use CTEs to avoid repeated work.\n• Update statistics; partition large tables; avoid unnecessary DISTINCT.\n• Compare plans/times before and after."},{"q":"How does partition pruning work and how do you enable it?","type":"concept","a":"Partition pruning means the engine reads only the partitions matching the filter, skipping the rest.\n• Filter directly on the partition column with range/equality predicates (WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31').\n• Avoid wrapping the partition column in functions (WHERE YEAR(order_date)=2025 disables pruning).\n• In Spark/Delta, this avoids reading irrelevant files/folders → big speedup."},{"q":"What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?","type":"concept","a":"• UNION — removes duplicate rows (implies a distinct/sort → more expensive).\n• UNION ALL — keeps all rows including duplicates (faster).\nUse UNION ALL by default and de-duplicate only when needed."},{"q":"What is a CTE and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"A Common Table Expression (WITH clause) is a named temporary result set that makes complex queries readable and avoids repeating subqueries. It's not materialized by default in many engines (each reference may re-execute), but improves clarity and can be chained. Use for multi-step logic: dedupe → join → aggregate."},{"q":"CODING: Find the manager of each employee using a self join (SQL).","type":"coding","a":"Self join employee to itself; LEFT join so top-level employees (null manager) are kept.","code":"SELECT e.empid AS employee_id, e.empname AS employee_name,\n       e.mgrid AS manager_id, m.empname AS manager_name\nFROM employee e\nLEFT JOIN employee m ON e.mgrid = m.empid;"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions and how do they differ from window functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Aggregates (SUM/COUNT/AVG/MIN/MAX) collapse multiple rows into one row per group — you lose individual rows.\n• Window functions compute across a set of rows but KEEP every row (add a computed column).\nAggregate example: SUM(sal) per dept. Window example: SUM(sal) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) alongside each row."},{"q":"How do you implement SCD Type 1 and Type 2 in SQL?","type":"coding","a":"Type 1 overwrites; Type 2 expires the old row and inserts a new version with effective dates + current flag.","code":"-- SCD Type 1 (overwrite)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.name=s.name, t.email=s.email, t.updated_at=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id,name,email,updated_at) VALUES (s.customer_id,s.name,s.email,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n\n-- SCD Type 2 (expire + insert)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s\nON t.customer_id = s.customer_id AND t.is_current = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (t.name<>s.name OR t.city<>s.city) THEN\n  UPDATE SET t.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, t.is_current = 0;\n\nINSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id,name,city,start_date,end_date,is_current)\nSELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1\nFROM staging s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id=s.customer_id AND d.is_current=1);"},{"q":"How would you find the Nth highest salary?","type":"concept","a":"Use DENSE_RANK (handles ties) or a correlated subquery.\n• Window: SELECT * FROM (SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) rk FROM emp) WHERE rk = N.\n• Subquery: SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp) [repeat for Nth].\nDENSE_RANK is cleaner and scales to any N."}]},"streaming":{"id":"streaming","name":"Streaming & Ingestion (Auto Loader, Event Hubs)","icon":"📡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Difference between batch and streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — bounded dataset processed on a schedule (hourly/daily); high throughput, higher latency; tools: Spark batch, ADF, SSIS.\n• Streaming — unbounded, continuously arriving data processed incrementally with low latency; tools: Spark Structured Streaming, Kafka, Event Hubs.\nChoose batch for EOD reports/historical loads; streaming for fraud detection, IoT, clickstream, real-time dashboards."},{"q":"What is Spark Structured Streaming?","type":"concept","a":"Structured Streaming is Spark's scalable, fault-tolerant stream engine built on the DataFrame/SQL API.\n• You write the same DataFrame transformations; Spark runs them incrementally as new data arrives.\n• Sources: Kafka, Event Hubs, Auto Loader (cloudFiles), Delta, files, sockets.\n• Sinks: Delta tables, files, foreachBatch, Kafka.\n• Guarantees: exactly-once via checkpointing + idempotent sinks.\nModel: output modes append / update / complete; triggers processingTime or availableNow."},{"q":"What is checkpointing and why is it important in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"Checkpointing persists stream progress (which offsets/data were processed) and state to durable storage (e.g., ADLS) so the stream can recover after failure and continue without reprocessing or losing data.\n• Enables fault tolerance and exactly-once processing.\n• option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/...').\n• Each stream needs its own checkpoint location; changing the query requires a new checkpoint (or careful migration)."},{"q":"What is watermarking in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"A watermark defines how long to wait for late-arriving events (bounded lateness). Spark retains state for aggregations/deduplication until the watermark passes, then drops old state to bound memory.\n• withWatermark('eventTime','10 minutes').\n• Events older than the watermark are treated as late and dropped from stateful ops.\n• Balances correctness (waiting for late data) vs resource usage."},{"q":"Difference between event time and processing time?","type":"concept","a":"• Event time — when the event actually occurred (timestamp in the data).\n• Processing time — when Spark processes/receives it.\nStreaming windows/watermarks should use EVENT time for correct results when data arrives out of order; processing time is simpler but inaccurate for late data."},{"q":"Explain window operations in Structured Streaming.","type":"concept","a":"• Tumbling window — fixed-size, non-overlapping (e.g., every 1 minute): window('ts','1 minute').\n• Sliding window — fixed-size, overlapping (e.g., 10-min window every 5 min): window('ts','10 minutes','5 minutes').\n• Session window — gaps-based (Spark 3.2+).\nUsed with groupBy + watermark for time-based aggregation over event time."},{"q":"What is Auto Loader (cloudFiles)?","type":"concept","a":"Auto Loader is a Structured Streaming source that incrementally and efficiently discovers and ingests NEW files from cloud storage into Delta.\n• Scales to billions of files; processes each file once (tracked via checkpoint + rocksdb state).\n• Schema inference + evolution; rescued data column captures malformed records.\n• Two discovery modes: directory listing (default) or file notification (Event Grid/Queue) for lower cost at scale.\nTypical: ADLS → Auto Loader → Bronze Delta → Silver → Gold.","code":"(spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles')\n  .option('cloudFiles.format','json')\n  .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation','/ckpt/schema')\n  .load('abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/')\n  .writeStream.format('delta')\n  .option('checkpointLocation','/ckpt/bronze')\n  .trigger(availableNow=True)\n  .toTable('bronze.events'))"},{"q":"What is the rescued data column in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"When incoming data doesn't match the expected schema (type mismatch, corrupt record), Auto Loader doesn't lose it — it stores the raw value in a special rescued data column (_rescued_data) as JSON.\n• Enables later triage/quarantine instead of dropping bad records.\n• Set via schemaHints/schemaLocation; default column name _rescued_data (configurable)."},{"q":"File notification vs directory listing in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Directory listing (default) — periodically lists the directory to find new files; simple, no extra Azure setup; cost grows with directory size.\n• File notification — subscribes to cloud events (Azure Event Grid + Storage Queue) to be notified of new files; more scalable and cheaper for high-volume/very large directories.\nChoose notification for scale; listing for simplicity/small volumes."},{"q":"How do you integrate Kafka or Event Hubs with Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Both expose a Kafka-compatible endpoint; use Spark's Kafka source.\n• readStream.format('kafka').option('kafka.bootstrap.servers',...).option('subscribe','topic').\n• For Event Hubs: use the Kafka API with Event Hubs connection string/SASL (or the native Event Hubs connector).\n• Parse the value (JSON) with from_json, then write to Delta with a checkpoint.\n• Deliver exactly-once by using Delta as the sink + checkpointing."},{"q":"How do you deduplicate streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"Use dropDuplicates on the streaming DataFrame with an event-time key, combined with a watermark to bound state:\ndf.withWatermark('event_ts','10 minutes').dropDuplicates(['id']).\nFor unbounded/all-time dedupe, use MERGE into a Delta table (whenNotMatchedInsert) or foreachBatch with a merge on the unique key."},{"q":"What trigger modes does Structured Streaming support?","type":"concept","a":"• Default/processingTime — micro-batches at intervals (e.g., '1 minute', '5 seconds').\n• availableNow — processes all available data as fast as possible then stops (efficient incremental batch; great for Auto Loader/COPY-style loads).\n• once (deprecated) — similar to availableNow.\n• continuous — experimental low-latency (~ms) mode with limited operators.\navailableNow is commonly used for cost-efficient incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is foreachBatch and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"foreachBatch lets you run arbitrary logic on each micro-batch as a batch DataFrame.\n• Use it when the sink isn't natively supported or you need MERGE/upsert, multiple sinks, or custom dedupe.\n• It handles idempotency per batch (use batchId + MERGE).\nSignature: df.writeStream.foreachBatch(lambda df, batch_id: ...)."},{"q":"How do you achieve exactly-once processing in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"• Use checkpointing to track offsets/state.\n• Use an idempotent/transactional sink — Delta Lake writes are atomic, so replay is safe.\n• With foreachBatch, key the MERGE on a stable business key so re-processing the same batch doesn't duplicate.\n• Combine replayable source (Kafka offsets) + atomic sink = exactly-once end-to-end."},{"q":"What is DLT / Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines and what are its key features?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Live Tables (now Databricks Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines) is a declarative framework for building reliable pipelines with dependency management.\n• Define @dlt.table functions; the engine builds the dependency graph automatically (no manual orchestration).\n• Streaming tables (incremental) + materialized views (refreshed).\n• Data quality EXPECTATIONS: expect, expect_or_drop, expect_or_fail.\n• Auto retries, automatic lineage, schema evolution, and managed compute.\nDLT evolved into Declarative Pipelines — same concepts, broader scope.","code":"import dlt\n@dlt.table\n@dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_amount', 'amount > 0')\ndef silver_orders():\n    return dlt.read_stream('bronze_orders').filter('status != \"CANCELLED\"')"},{"q":"Difference between Delta Live Tables and Databricks Declarative Pipelines?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables is the older name; Declarative Pipelines is the newer, broader framework from Databricks.\n• Same core concepts (declarative @dlt tables, expectations, auto dependency, streaming tables + materialized views) with additional capabilities and general data-pipeline scope beyond ETL.\nSimple answer: DLT has evolved into Databricks Declarative Pipelines."},{"q":"How do you design a real-time analytics pipeline (Event Hubs → Databricks → Delta → BI)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Ingest: Spark Structured Streaming reads from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse (from_json), apply watermarks + dedupe, transform aggregations.\n• Write to a Bronze streaming Delta table (or DLT streaming table) with checkpointing for exactly-once.\n• Silver cleans/enriches; Gold aggregates for serving.\n• Serve to Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse, or to Real-Time Analytics for millisecond dashboards.\n• Monitor lag, throughput, and checkpoint health; tune triggers/partitions."},{"q":"What are the modes for writing streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"• Append — only new rows (default; Delta table grows).\n• Update — emit updated aggregation results (no full recompute).\n• Complete — rewrite the entire result table each batch (for aggregations without watermark).\nMost Delta streaming sinks use append; aggregations use update/complete."}]}};
